window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("enterprise-integration", {"topics":[{"key":"enterprise-integration/enterprise-connectors","sources":["Official docs: Microsoft Graph (delta query, SharePoint and Teams resources)","Official docs: Salesforce REST, Bulk and Change Data Capture APIs","Official docs: Atlassian Confluence and Jira Cloud REST APIs","Official docs: Slack Web API and Events API"],"why":"Enterprise knowledge lives in a dozen SaaS and on-prem systems, each with its own API, object model, query\nlanguage, auth scheme and permission semantics. A RAG assistant or agent is only as useful as the systems\nit can see, so \"can it read our Confluence and Jira?\" is usually the first question in a deal.\n\nThe naive approach is a script per source: authenticate with an admin token, page through everything,\ndump the text into a vector store. It demos well and fails in production for four reasons. First, it reads\nwith admin rights and discards the source ACLs, so the assistant happily answers a junior employee's\nquestion with a page from a restricted HR space. Second, re-fetching everything nightly burns through API\nquotas that the customer's other integrations also depend on, and gets throttled. Third, it never notices\ndeletions, so documents removed for legal reasons keep surfacing. Fourth, it hard-codes one tenant's quirks,\nso the second customer breaks it. A connector is the disciplined version: a repeatable contract for\nauth, enumeration, change tracking, permissions and normalization that every source implements.","how":"### The connector contract\nEvery connector, whatever the source, implements the same five responsibilities, *because* the downstream\npipeline (chunking, embedding, ACL-aware retrieval, tool calling) should not care where a document came from:\n\n1. **Authenticate** as an app registered in the customer's tenant (OAuth client credentials, a service\n   account, or delegated user tokens), with the narrowest scopes that work.\n2. **Enumerate** the containers in scope (sites, spaces, projects, channels, objects) and page through\n   items, honouring pagination cursors and rate-limit responses (HTTP 429, `Retry-After`).\n3. **Track change**: store a cursor (delta token, change-event replay ID, or `updated` watermark) so the next\n   run fetches only what changed, including deletions.\n4. **Capture permissions** alongside content: who can see this item, expressed as source principals that can\n   later be resolved to users.\n5. **Normalize** into a canonical document (id, source URL, title, body, author, timestamps, ACL, source\n   metadata) so one indexing pipeline serves all sources.\n\n### What differs per system\n- **Salesforce.** Records are queried with SOQL over the REST API; large extracts go through the Bulk API\n  because per-record REST calls burn the org's API allocation. Change is available via Change Data Capture\n  events or `SystemModstamp` watermarks. Visibility is computed from profiles, permission sets, role\n  hierarchy and sharing rules, which is too complex to replicate faithfully, so many connectors restrict\n  scope to objects with simple sharing or check access at query time.\n- **SAP.** Access is typically through OData services (S/4HANA, SAP Gateway), RFC/BAPI calls, or IDoc\n  messages for asynchronous document exchange. Data is structured transactional records rather than prose,\n  so the connector is more often a tool an agent calls (look up an order, check stock) than a corpus to\n  embed. Authorization objects are fine-grained and usually enforced by calling SAP as the user.\n- **ServiceNow.** The Table API exposes records (incidents, knowledge articles, CMDB items) with encoded\n  queries. ACLs are scripted per table and field, so a service account's view is not a user's view.\n- **SharePoint and Teams.** Both are reached through Microsoft Graph. Drive and list items support delta\n  queries that return changes and deletions since a token. Permissions are per site, library, folder and\n  item, with inheritance that can be broken at any level. Teams channel messages are also in Graph; some\n  message-export APIs are classed as protected and require an approval or licensing step, so check before\n  promising them.\n- **Confluence and Jira.** Atlassian REST APIs with CQL and JQL for querying by `lastmodified` or `updated`.\n  Confluence combines space permissions with page restrictions that inherit down the page tree; Jira uses\n  project permission schemes plus optional issue security levels.\n- **Slack.** The Web API (`conversations.history`, `conversations.replies`) for backfill, the Events API for\n  live changes, with method-specific rate-limit tiers. Slack has tightened history-read limits for some\n  classes of apps (the 2025 changes for apps not distributed through its Marketplace), so check the\n  current limits for your app type before sizing a backfill. Visibility is channel membership, which changes\n  often, and private channels and DMs are only visible to apps that have been added or granted the right scopes.\n\n### From source principals to people\nThe ACL a connector captures is written in the source's own vocabulary: SharePoint and Teams use Entra\nobject IDs plus SharePoint site groups, Confluence and Jira use Atlassian account IDs and groups, Slack uses\nits own user IDs and channel membership, Salesforce uses user IDs, roles and public groups. None of these is\nthe identity the user signs in with. The connector therefore also maintains a **principal crosswalk**:\nsource principal ID to the IdP's immutable user or group ID, built from each source's user directory API\nand joined on the IdP object ID where the source records it (Microsoft sources do) or on a verified email\nwhere it does not. Source-local groups are expanded to members, nested groups recursively, *because* the\nretrieval filter can only test identities it knows. Two special cases need explicit modelling rather than\ndropping: sharing links (\"anyone in the organization with the link\", anonymous links), which grant access\nto a scope rather than to a listed principal, and guest or external users with no IdP account. A principal\nthat resolves to nobody must fail closed (it grants nothing), not be ignored in a way that widens access.\n\n### Sizing the first crawl\nThe initial backfill, not the steady state, decides the project plan. Take an illustrative SharePoint\ntenant with 2 million files: one content download plus one permissions call per file is about 4 million\nrequests. At a sustained 10 requests per second (an illustrative rate; the real one depends on the\nsource's throttling for your app and tenant) that is 400,000 seconds, roughly four and a half days, and\nevery retry after a 429 extends it. The levers follow from the arithmetic: batch requests where the API\nallows it (Graph JSON batching groups up to 20 requests per call, though throttling still counts the\nindividual requests), read permissions once per folder whose inheritance is intact instead of once per\nfile, crawl the highest-value containers first so the pilot is useful before the crawl finishes, and make\nthe crawl resumable by checkpointing per container. After the backfill, cost scales with the change rate,\nwhich is typically a small fraction of the corpus per day.\n\n### Build, buy or use MCP\nUnified-API vendors and the AI platforms' own connector catalogues cover the common sources; building\nyour own makes sense when you need permissions, fields or freshness they do not provide. For agent actions,\nwrapping each system as an MCP server gives a standard tool interface, but it does not remove any of the\nauth, quota or permission work above.","example":"A SharePoint document-library connector using Microsoft Graph delta queries. The first call returns every\nitem; subsequent calls with the saved `deltaLink` return only changes, and deleted items carry a `deleted`\nfacet, which is how the connector removes them from the index.\n\n```python\nimport time, requests\n\nGRAPH = \"https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0\"\n\ndef sync_drive(token: str, drive_id: str, saved_delta: str | None, index) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Pull changes since saved_delta into `index`; return the new delta link to persist.\"\"\"\n    url = saved_delta or f\"{GRAPH}/drives/{drive_id}/root/delta\"\n    headers = {\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"}\n    while True:\n        r = requests.get(url, headers=headers, timeout=30)\n        if r.status_code == 429:                       # throttled: honour the server's hint\n            time.sleep(int(r.headers.get(\"Retry-After\", \"10\")))\n            continue\n        r.raise_for_status()\n        page = r.json()\n        for item in page[\"value\"]:\n            key = f\"sharepoint:{drive_id}:{item['id']}\"\n            if \"deleted\" in item:\n                index.delete(key)                      # deletions arrive as tombstones\n            elif \"file\" in item:\n                perms = requests.get(f\"{GRAPH}/drives/{drive_id}/items/{item['id']}/permissions\",\n                                     headers=headers, timeout=30).json()[\"value\"]\n                index.upsert(key, {\n                    \"title\": item[\"name\"],\n                    \"url\": item[\"webUrl\"],\n                    \"modified\": item[\"lastModifiedDateTime\"],\n                    # sharing links carry a \"link\" facet with a scope instead of a named principal\n                    \"acl\": [p.get(\"grantedToV2\") or p.get(\"grantedToIdentitiesV2\")\n                            or {\"link\": p.get(\"link\", {}).get(\"scope\")} for p in perms],\n                })                                     # content download + parsing happens downstream\n        if \"@odata.nextLink\" in page:\n            url = page[\"@odata.nextLink\"]              # more pages in this round\n        else:\n            return page[\"@odata.deltaLink\"]            # persist; next run starts here\n```\n\nNote what the code commits to: one permissions call per changed file (fine for incremental runs, expensive\nfor a 2-million-file initial crawl, which is where batching and a separate permission-sync job come in;\nthe permissions call also needs the same 429 handling as the delta call, omitted here for length).\nThe ordering matters too: the `deltaLink` is persisted only after the last page is processed, so a crash\nmid-round replays the round from the previous token, and the upserts and deletes are idempotent by key,\nso replaying them is harmless.\nIt also only re-reads permissions for items the delta reports: by default a delta round surfaces content and\nmetadata changes, and a sharing change on a folder does not list every descendant whose effective access\nchanged. Graph has request options for reporting sharing changes on business drives; without them, or in\naddition to them, run a periodic permission resync.","tradeoffs":["Copying content into your own index gives fast, rankable retrieval, but creates a second copy whose permissions and freshness you must keep in sync; federated live search through source APIs avoids that copy and flips the cost to latency and per-query rate limits.","Application-level service accounts make crawling simple, but they see more than any user, so you must replicate ACLs; delegated user tokens enforce source permissions natively but see only that one user's slice and stop working when the user's refresh token or consent is revoked.","Buying a connector platform gets twenty sources quickly, but you inherit its permission fidelity and sync cadence; building wins when one source is business-critical and needs field-level control.","Syncing permissions at index time makes queries cheap, but permission changes lag by one sync cycle; checking access live at query time is exact but adds a source API call per candidate document."],"pitfalls":["Crawling with an admin token and dropping ACLs; the symptom is a user reporting the assistant quoted a document they cannot open in the source system.","Re-fetching full corpora nightly instead of using delta cursors; the symptom is HTTP 429s and the customer's other integrations failing during your crawl window.","Ignoring deletions and permission removals; the symptom is retrieval returning links that 404 or show \"access denied\" in the source.","Treating broken permission inheritance as rare in SharePoint or Confluence; the symptom is a few sensitive folders indexed with their parent site's wider audience.","Joining source users to IdP users by display name or an unverified email; the symptom is ACL entries that resolve to nobody (users see less than in the source) or to the wrong person after a name collision.","Dropping sharing-link permissions because they name no principal; the symptom is documents shared \"with the organization\" being invisible to everyone, or, if mapped carelessly, visible to guests.","Promising a source (Teams messages, Slack DMs, a SAP module) before checking the API scope, approval or licence it needs; the symptom is a blocked project week three."],"when":"Build or adopt a connector whenever an AI feature must read or act on a customer system more than once.\nFor a one-off pilot, an export file may be enough; for production, insist on delta sync, deletion handling\nand permission capture from day one, because retrofitting ACLs onto a populated index means re-crawling it.\nBefore committing to a timeline, size the backfill: item count times calls per item divided by the\nsustained rate the source will actually give you. Measure sync lag (source change to searchable), API calls\nper sync and share of the customer's quota, 429 rate, permission-mismatch rate from spot checks as\nrestricted test users, unresolved-principal count, and items in error. In interviews, the expected depth is naming the five responsibilities and explaining\nhow one specific source models permissions and change.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the connector contract per source: OAuth app, paging with backoff, delta cursors persisted per container, tombstone handling and ACL extraction into the canonical document.","architect":"Decides copy-and-index versus federated query per source, build versus buy, and where permission enforcement lives so every connector feeds one ACL-aware retrieval path.","fde":"Gets the app registered and admin consent granted in the customer tenant, negotiates API quota with the customer's admins and verifies permissions on real restricted spaces before go-live."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants their assistant to answer from Confluence and Slack. Security asks how you prevent\na user from seeing content they cannot see in the source. What is your design?","a":"Capture permissions as source principals during sync: Confluence space permissions plus inherited page\nrestrictions, Slack channel membership. Resolve principals to users through the customer IdP (groups via\nSCIM or Graph) and store them on each chunk. At query time, filter retrieval by the requesting user's\nresolved principal set before ranking, never after generation. Because Slack membership changes often, sync\nmembership more frequently than content, or check membership live for private channels. Log every answer's\nsource documents so access can be audited, and spot-check by querying as a restricted test user."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do production connectors use delta tokens or change events rather than nightly full crawls?","options":["Full crawls cannot read file contents","They fetch only changes and deletions, staying within API quotas and noticing removals","Delta tokens make the content more accurate for embedding","Source systems forbid full crawls after the first one"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A full crawl repeats work, competes for the customer's shared API allocation and cannot easily see what disappeared. Delta mechanisms return just the changes, including deletion tombstones, so the index stays fresh and consistent cheaply."},{"q":"A connector indexes SharePoint using an app-only token with read access to all sites. What must it add to be safe?","options":["A higher rate limit so crawling finishes faster","Encryption of the vector store at rest","Per-item permissions captured at sync and enforced as a retrieval filter for the requesting user","A rule that only site owners may use the assistant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An app-only token sees everything, so the index must carry each item's ACL, including broken inheritance, and filter by the user's principals. Encryption protects storage but does nothing about authorized users seeing too much."},{"q":"Why is SAP more often integrated as an agent tool than as a document corpus?","options":["SAP has no API of any kind","SAP data is always encrypted and cannot be read","Its data is structured transactional records with fine-grained authorization best enforced by calling SAP itself","Embedding models cannot process German text"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Orders, materials and invoices are rows, not prose, and change constantly; querying them live through OData or BAPI calls returns current values and lets SAP's own authorization objects apply, which embedding snapshots would bypass."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/legacy-system-integration","sources":["Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns","Evans, 2003 — Domain-Driven Design","Fowler, 2004 — StranglerFigApplication (bliki)"],"why":"In most large enterprises the system of record for policies, accounts, claims or inventory is decades old.\nIt works, it is audited, and nobody will rewrite it for your assistant. Yet \"what is the status of claim\n4471?\" or \"raise a return for this order\" can only be answered by that system.\n\nThe naive approaches each break something. Querying the production database directly with ad-hoc SQL\ncompetes with the batch jobs and online transactions it was sized for, and on a mainframe, CPU consumption\ncan translate directly into the customer's software bill. Writing to its tables bypasses the business rules\nthat live in application code, corrupting state in ways nobody notices until month-end. Copying its data\nmodel straight into your service (cryptic eight-character field names, status code `07`, dates as\n`YYDDD`) spreads legacy semantics everywhere, so every later change touches every consumer. And treating it\nlike a cloud API (retry instantly, call it per user request, expect sub-second latency) collides with\nnightly batch windows, single-threaded transaction monitors and maintenance freezes. Legacy integration is\nthe discipline of extracting value without destabilizing the thing that runs the business.","how":"### 1. Choose the access path, least invasive first\nOrdered roughly from safest to riskiest, *because* each step down couples you more tightly to internals:\n- **Existing interface.** A SOAP service, an MQ queue (IBM MQ is common on mainframes), a published batch\n  extract, or an ESB/iPaaS flow someone already maintains. Reusing it inherits its rules and its support.\n- **Batch file exchange.** Fixed-width or delimited files over SFTP on a schedule. Crude but decoupled: the\n  legacy side controls load, and files are replayable.\n- **Read replica or CDC.** Read from a replica, or capture changes from the database log, so reads never touch\n  the primary. Writes still go through the application.\n- **Transaction wrappers.** Expose an existing transaction program (for example a CICS transaction) through a\n  gateway, so business rules still run on writes.\n- **Screen automation (RPA).** Drive the green-screen or desktop UI. It is the last resort *because* any UI\n  change breaks it, but sometimes it is the only path that runs the full validation logic.\n\n### 2. Build an anti-corruption layer\nA translation service owned by your side converts legacy records into your canonical model and back. It\ndecodes encodings (EBCDIC, COBOL `COMP-3` packed decimal, implied decimal points), maps status codes to\nnamed states, normalizes dates and time zones, and hides access-path details behind domain operations such\nas `get_claim(id)`. Consumers, including LLM tools, see only that interface, *because* when the legacy\nsystem is eventually replaced, only this layer changes.\n\nTwo encoding traps account for most \"the numbers are wrong\" incidents. Signed numeric fields stored as\ndisplay digits (`PIC S9(5)V99` without `COMP-3`) carry the sign in the zone half of the last byte, so the\nfinal character looks like a letter or brace after conversion rather than a digit. And mixed records,\ntext fields beside packed binary fields, must be transferred as binary and decoded field by field from\nthe copybook, *because* a file-transfer step that converts the whole file from EBCDIC to ASCII rewrites the\npacked bytes as if they were characters and destroys them without any error.\n\n### 3. Protect the legacy system\nPut a cache or replica in front of reads, a queue in front of writes, and explicit concurrency limits\neverywhere. Size the limit with Little's law: concurrent calls = arrival rate × call latency. If an agent\nfeature generates an illustrative 20 lookups per second and each legacy transaction takes 1.5 seconds,\nabout 30 calls are in flight at once; if the operations team will allow 10 concurrent sessions, you must\ncache, batch, or shed load, *because* the gateway will otherwise queue requests until user-facing\ntimeouts fire and retries make it worse. Respect batch windows: schedule bulk extracts when the system says, and make agent tools return\n\"system unavailable, try after 06:00\" rather than hammering it.\n\n### 4. Make writes safe\nLegacy transactions are rarely idempotent, and a timeout has three possible meanings: the request never\narrived, it ran and the reply was lost, or it is still running. Retrying blindly is only safe in the first\ncase. So attach a client reference to every write, record it in your own store before sending, and put it\nin a field the legacy system persists and can be queried by (a customer reference or batch reference\nfield, agreed with the system's owners). After a timeout, look the reference up before retrying; if it\nexists, the write happened. If the system offers no queryable field, route writes through a single queue\nconsumer and confirm them against the next extract, *because* only reconciliation can then tell you which\nin-doubt writes landed.\n\n### 5. Migrate incrementally if replacing\nThe strangler fig pattern routes one capability at a time from the legacy system to the new one behind a\nfacade, *because* big-bang rewrites of systems with decades of undocumented rules routinely fail.","example":"A nightly SFTP extract contains fixed-width 30-byte records in EBCDIC: customer id `PIC X(10)`, name\n`PIC X(16)`, balance `PIC S9(5)V99 COMP-3` (seven digits plus a sign nibble = 4 bytes, two implied decimals).\nThe anti-corruption layer decodes it into a typed record.\n\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Customer:\n    customer_id: str\n    name: str\n    balance: Decimal\n\ndef unpack_comp3(raw: bytes, scale: int) -> Decimal:\n    \"\"\"COBOL packed decimal: two digits per byte, last nibble is the sign (C/F positive, D negative).\"\"\"\n    nibbles = []\n    for b in raw:\n        nibbles += [b >> 4, b & 0x0F]\n    sign = nibbles.pop()                       # final nibble\n    if sign < 0x0A or any(n > 9 for n in nibbles):   # A-F are signs, 0-9 digits\n        raise ValueError(f\"corrupt packed field {raw.hex()}\")\n    value = int(\"\".join(map(str, nibbles)))\n    if sign in (0x0B, 0x0D):                   # D preferred negative, B alternate\n        value = -value\n    return Decimal(value).scaleb(-scale)\n\ndef parse_record(rec: bytes) -> Customer:\n    assert len(rec) == 30, len(rec)\n    return Customer(\n        customer_id=rec[0:10].decode(\"cp037\").strip(),   # cp037 = EBCDIC US/Canada\n        name=rec[10:26].decode(\"cp037\").strip(),\n        balance=unpack_comp3(rec[26:30], scale=2),\n    )\n\n# 12345.67 packs to 12 34 56 7C; -1.50 packs to 00 00 15 0D\nassert unpack_comp3(bytes.fromhex(\"1234567C\"), 2) == Decimal(\"12345.67\")\nassert unpack_comp3(bytes.fromhex(\"0000150D\"), 2) == Decimal(\"-1.50\")\n```\n\nReading the file as UTF-8 would raise decode errors or produce garbage names and, worse, treating the packed\nbytes as text (or decoding them with `cp037`, which maps every byte to some character) would\nsilently produce plausible-looking wrong balances, which is why the layout (the COBOL copybook) is the first\nartifact to request from the customer.","tradeoffs":["Batch file exchange is decoupled and replayable, but data is as stale as the last extract; it flips to CDC or a queue when users need answers about changes made in the last hour.","Reading a replica protects the primary, but replication lag means a just-submitted transaction may be invisible; read-your-writes flows must query the primary or wait.","Screen automation reaches logic no API exposes, but breaks on any UI change and is slow; it is acceptable only when volumes are low and a proper interface is on the roadmap.","An anti-corruption layer costs a service to build and run, but without it every consumer learns legacy codes; it stops paying off only for a single throwaway consumer."],"pitfalls":["Querying the production database during the batch window; the symptom is the nightly job overrunning and the operations team asking who is running unplanned queries.","Decoding with the wrong code page or ignoring packed decimals; the symptom is mojibake in names or balances off by orders of magnitude.","Retrying non-idempotent legacy writes on timeout; the symptom is duplicate orders or payments discovered at reconciliation.","Exposing raw legacy codes to the LLM as tool output; the symptom is the model guessing what status `07` means and confidently telling a customer the wrong thing.","Transferring mixed text-and-packed files in text mode, or letting a gateway convert code pages for the whole record; the symptom is names that look fine while numeric fields are garbage or fail validation.","Assuming 24/7 availability; the symptom is agent tools failing every night during maintenance with unhelpful error messages."],"when":"Use these patterns whenever an AI feature must read from or write to a system you cannot change. Start by\nasking for the existing interfaces, the batch schedule, the record layouts and the capacity constraints,\nbefore writing code. Prefer read-only integration for a first release; add writes only through a path that\nruns the system's own business rules, with idempotency references. Measure data freshness against the batch schedule, load placed on the legacy system (calls, concurrency,\nCPU where the owners can report it), in-doubt writes and how they resolved, parse rejects per extract, and\nreconciliation mismatches. In interviews, explaining why you would not point an agent\nat the mainframe database directly, and what you would do instead, is the core signal.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the anti-corruption layer: copybook-driven parsers, code-to-state mappings, idempotent write wrappers and concurrency limits around legacy calls.","architect":"Chooses the access path per capability, sets the boundary where legacy semantics stop, and plans strangler-style replacement rather than coupling new AI features to internals.","fde":"Finds the person who knows the batch schedule and copybooks, gets SFTP or MQ access approved, and validates parsed records against what the legacy screens show."},"check":{"q":"An insurer wants an agent that can update a claim's contact address. The claims system is a mainframe\nwith no API, a nightly batch and a green-screen UI. How do you approach it?","a":"First look for an existing interface: an MQ-fed transaction, a CICS transaction exposed through a\ngateway, or a batch update file the system already ingests. Any of these runs the system's own validation.\nPrefer them over direct table writes, which bypass rules, and over screen automation, which is brittle.\nWrap the chosen path in an anti-corruption layer with a domain operation like `update_contact(claim_id,\naddress, ref)`, record the reference before sending and, after a timeout, look it up in the claims system before\nretrying, so an in-doubt write is never applied twice, and queue requests so the\nagent never overloads the system or calls during batch. If only a nightly file exists, make the agent say\nthe change is scheduled, not done."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is screen automation usually the last-resort integration path?","options":["It cannot read data, only write it","Any UI change breaks it and it is slow, even though it does run the application's business rules","Mainframes do not allow terminal sessions","It requires rewriting the legacy application"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Driving the UI does pass through full validation logic, which is its appeal, but it couples you to screen layouts that change without notice and runs at human interaction speed, so it suits low volumes only."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of an anti-corruption layer?","options":["To encrypt legacy data before it leaves the data centre","To replace the legacy system with a modern one","To translate the legacy model into your own so legacy semantics do not spread into every consumer","To filter malicious prompts before they reach the mainframe"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The layer decodes formats, maps codes to named states and exposes domain operations, so only it changes when the legacy system changes or is replaced; replacement itself is the strangler fig's job."},{"q":"A packed-decimal balance field is read as text. What is the most likely outcome?","options":["An exception is always raised immediately","The value is correct but loses its sign","Garbled or plausible-but-wrong values flow through unless the parser validates the field","The database rejects the record"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Packed decimal stores two digits per byte plus a sign nibble, so text decoding yields nonsense characters or numbers that look valid; explicit COMP-3 decoding with nibble validation is required."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/sso-iam-integration","sources":["Official docs: Microsoft identity platform (Entra ID)","Official docs: Okta Developer","OpenID Foundation, 2014 — OpenID Connect Core 1.0"],"why":"An enterprise has thousands of employees joining, moving and leaving. Its security team enforces MFA,\ndevice posture and conditional access in one place, the IdP, and audits who can reach which application.\nA vendor with its own username/password store breaks every one of those controls: leavers keep access,\nMFA policy is bypassed, and passwords get reused. So \"supports SSO with our Okta / Entra tenant\" is a\nprocurement checkbox, often a hard gate.\n\nThe naive implementation is \"add a Sign in with Microsoft button\". It works for one tenant and fails as a\nB2B product. Accepting any Entra token means any organization's users can sign in to any customer's\nworkspace. Keying users by email breaks when someone's address changes or a mailbox is reassigned to a new\nhire. Reading group names from tokens fails when groups are renamed, or when a user is in so many groups\nthat the token omits them. And signing someone in once tells you nothing about the day they are fired: your\nsession keeps working unless you wire deprovisioning in. SSO integration is the set of decisions that make\nfederated identity safe across many customer tenants.","how":"### Roles and protocols\nThe customer's IdP authenticates the user; your application is the relying party (OIDC) or service\nprovider (SAML). OIDC returns a signed JWT ID token and is the default for new integrations; SAML is\nXML-based and still required by many enterprises (see the SAML lesson). Both Okta and Entra ID support both.\n\n### The login flow (OIDC authorization code with PKCE)\n1. Your app redirects the browser to the tenant's authorization endpoint with `client_id`, `redirect_uri`,\n   `scope=openid profile email`, a random `state`, a random `nonce`, and a PKCE `code_challenge`. Store\n   `state`, `nonce` and the PKCE verifier in the user's pre-login session, *because* each must later be\n   matched against *this* browser: `state` defeats login CSRF, `nonce` ties the ID token to this request,\n   PKCE stops a stolen authorization code from being redeemed by someone else.\n2. The IdP authenticates the user and applies its policies (MFA, device compliance, conditional access),\n   then redirects back with a short-lived `code`.\n3. Your server exchanges the code at the token endpoint, authenticating as the client (secret or, better,\n   a certificate or private-key JWT), and receives an ID token (and an access token if you asked for API scopes).\n4. You validate the ID token (below), resolve the user, and issue **your own** session. The IdP's tokens\n   are not your session, *because* their lifetime and revocation are controlled elsewhere and you still\n   need your own idle and absolute timeouts.\n\nIf a tenant requires stronger authentication for some actions, the ID token's `acr`/`amr` claims say how\nthe user authenticated, and you can send them back to the IdP for step-up rather than trusting a stale login.\n\n### Per-tenant configuration\nStore, per customer tenant: protocol, issuer (or SAML entity ID), client ID, discovery or metadata URL,\nallowed email domains, and claim mappings. *Because* each customer brings its own IdP, the login flow starts\nwith home realm discovery: the user types an email or picks a workspace, and you route them to that tenant's\nIdP. In Entra ID, a SaaS app is typically registered once as multi-tenant and each customer's admin grants\nconsent; in Okta, each customer org adds your app (often from the Okta Integration Network) and you receive\nan org-specific issuer. Okta also distinguishes the org authorization server (issuer\n`https://<org>.okta.com`) from custom authorization servers (issuer `https://<org>.okta.com/oauth2/<id>`,\n`default` being the common one); they have different issuers and keys, so record which one the customer\nconfigured rather than assuming.\n\n### Validating the token\nFor every ID token: verify the signature against the issuer's JWKS, check `iss` equals the configured issuer\nfor *this* tenant, `aud` equals your client ID, `exp`/`nbf` with small clock skew, and the `nonce` you sent.\nFor Entra multi-tenant apps the issuer contains the tenant ID (`tid`), so you must check that `tid` belongs\nto a customer you onboarded; signature validity alone proves only that *some* Entra tenant issued it. This\nis a common hole, *because* the multi-tenant discovery document (`/organizations` or `/common`) advertises\nan issuer containing a `{tenantid}` placeholder, generic libraries cannot compare against it literally, and\nthe tempting fix is to switch issuer validation off. The correct fix is to substitute the onboarded tenant's\nID and compare exactly.\n\n### Stable identity\nKey users by issuer plus `sub`, or in Entra by `tid` plus `oid`, *because* those are immutable for the\naccount; email and UPN are display attributes that change.\n\n### Authorization inputs\nPrefer app roles (Entra) or a filtered groups claim (Okta) that the customer's admin assigns, over raw\ngroup lists. Entra caps the groups it puts in a token (its documentation gives 200 for JWTs and 150 for\nSAML assertions); above the cap it omits them and signals an overage (`_claim_names`/`_claim_sources` in a\nJWT), in which case you must fetch membership from Microsoft Graph. Configuring the app to emit only groups\nassigned to it avoids most overage, *because* users rarely hold more than a few of those. Map group or role *IDs*, not display names.\n\n### Lifecycle and delegation\nSSO controls sign-in, not existence. Pair it with SCIM so deactivation reaches you, and keep your session\nlifetime short enough that a disabled user cannot ride an old cookie for days. The worst-case exposure after\ntermination is roughly the time for SCIM deactivation to arrive plus whatever your app does not revoke on\nreceipt; if you revoke sessions on SCIM events, session length matters only as a backstop. Where the IdP\nsupports OIDC back-channel logout, it can also tell you to end a session directly. When an agent calls downstream\nAPIs as the user, use a delegation flow such as Entra's on-behalf-of or OAuth token exchange, so the\ndownstream system sees the user's identity and scopes rather than a god-mode service account.","example":"Validating OIDC ID tokens for many customer tenants with PyJWT. The tenant record comes from your\nonboarding table; the issuer and JWKS come from that tenant's discovery document.\n\n```python\nimport jwt, requests\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n\nTENANTS = {   # from your DB, written during customer onboarding\n    \"acme\":   {\"issuer\": \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<acme-tenant-id>/v2.0\",\n               \"client_id\": \"<our-app-client-id>\"},\n    \"globex\": {\"issuer\": \"https://globex.okta.com/oauth2/default\",\n               \"client_id\": \"<our-okta-client-id>\"},\n}\n\n@lru_cache(maxsize=256)\ndef jwks_client(issuer: str) -> jwt.PyJWKClient:\n    conf = requests.get(f\"{issuer}/.well-known/openid-configuration\", timeout=10).json()\n    return jwt.PyJWKClient(conf[\"jwks_uri\"])\n\ndef validate_id_token(tenant_slug: str, token: str, expected_nonce: str) -> dict:\n    t = TENANTS[tenant_slug]                       # routed by home realm discovery, never by the token\n    key = jwks_client(t[\"issuer\"]).get_signing_key_from_jwt(token).key\n    claims = jwt.decode(\n        token, key, algorithms=[\"RS256\"],\n        audience=t[\"client_id\"], issuer=t[\"issuer\"],  # rejects tokens from any other tenant\n        leeway=60, options={\"require\": [\"exp\", \"iat\", \"iss\", \"aud\", \"sub\"]},\n    )\n    if claims.get(\"nonce\") != expected_nonce:\n        raise PermissionError(\"nonce mismatch (possible replay)\")\n    user_key = (claims[\"iss\"], claims.get(\"oid\") or claims[\"sub\"])  # stable, not email\n    return {\"user_key\": user_key, \"email\": claims.get(\"email\"),\n            \"roles\": claims.get(\"roles\", []), \"groups\": claims.get(\"groups\", [])}\n```\n\nThe crucial line is `issuer=t[\"issuer\"]` chosen from the tenant the user was routed to: a valid token from\nGlobex's Okta presented to Acme's workspace fails validation instead of logging someone in.","tradeoffs":["OIDC is simpler to implement and debug than SAML, but some customers' IdP policies or legacy setups mandate SAML; supporting both doubles the test matrix, so add SAML when the first deal requires it.","Just-in-time provisioning on first login needs no extra integration, but never learns about leavers; SCIM adds that at the cost of another endpoint and per-IdP quirks.","Emitting groups in tokens avoids extra calls, but large memberships bloat tokens or trigger overage; app roles assigned by the customer admin keep tokens small and intent explicit.","Short application sessions make deprovisioning effective quickly, but force more IdP round-trips; long sessions are smoother and acceptable only when SCIM revocation kills sessions immediately."],"pitfalls":["Accepting any Entra tenant's token in a multi-tenant app, often by disabling issuer validation to get past the `{tenantid}` placeholder; the symptom is a user from an unrelated company successfully reaching a customer workspace in a penetration test.","Using email as the primary key; the symptom is a rehired or renamed employee getting a second account, or a new hire inheriting a predecessor's history.","Mapping permissions by group display name; the symptom is access silently disappearing after the customer renames a group.","Ignoring the groups overage case; the symptom is power users with many memberships having no permissions while everyone else works.","Letting an agent call downstream systems with a shared service credential; the symptom is audit logs where every action belongs to one bot account."],"when":"Any B2B product sold to companies with more than a handful of users needs IdP integration; plan it before\nthe first enterprise deal, not after. Start with OIDC plus per-tenant issuer validation, add SAML and SCIM as\ncustomers require, and route authorization through a mapping layer rather than raw claims. Measure login\nsuccess rate per tenant, time from IdP deactivation to loss of access, and token-validation failures by\nreason. In interviews, the signal is knowing that SSO is authentication only, and explaining tenant-bound\nissuer checks, stable keys and deprovisioning.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements discovery, JWKS caching, issuer and audience checks per tenant, stable user keys, and the groups-overage fallback to Microsoft Graph.","architect":"Owns the identity model: which protocols are supported, where tenant-to-IdP mapping lives, session lifetime policy and how agent delegation preserves the user's identity downstream.","fde":"Walks the customer's IAM admin through app registration, consent, claim configuration and test users, then verifies a deactivated test account loses access within the agreed time."},"check":{"q":"Your multi-tenant app accepts Entra ID tokens. A pen tester signs in to a customer's workspace using an\naccount from their own personal Entra tenant. What went wrong and how do you fix it?","a":"The app verified the signature against Microsoft's common keys and the audience, but did not bind the\ntoken to the customer's tenant. Every Entra tenant's tokens are signed by the same key set, so a valid\nsignature proves nothing about which organization issued it. The fix is to resolve the tenant from the login\nroute (workspace or email domain), then require `tid` and `iss` to match the tenant ID stored at onboarding,\nand to key users by `tid` plus `oid`. Add a regression test with a token from an unregistered tenant."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should users be keyed by issuer plus subject (or tid plus oid) rather than email?","options":["Email addresses are too long to index","Subject and object IDs are immutable for the account, while emails change and can be reassigned","OIDC tokens never contain an email claim","Email keys break the token signature"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Immutable identifiers keep one account per person across renames and prevent a recycled mailbox from inheriting someone else's data; email is a mutable display attribute, though usually present in tokens."},{"q":"What does SSO by itself fail to handle when an employee leaves the customer?","options":["MFA enforcement on their next login","Revoking their access at the IdP","Ending sessions and access already established in your application","Signing the ID tokens they were issued"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Disabling the user at the IdP blocks new sign-ins, but your existing session lives on until it expires; SCIM deactivation plus session revocation or short sessions closes that gap."},{"q":"An Entra user in many groups has an empty groups claim. What is the likely cause?","options":["The app registration is single-tenant","The token signature failed verification","Group overage: Entra omitted the groups and expects the app to query Microsoft Graph for membership","SCIM has not run yet"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When membership exceeds the token's limit, Entra sends an overage indicator instead of the list; apps must fall back to Graph, or better, use app roles assigned by the admin to keep tokens small."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/saml-sso","sources":["OASIS, 2005 — Security Assertion Markup Language (SAML) V2.0 Core and Bindings","Somorovsky et al., 2012 — On Breaking SAML: Be Whoever You Want to Be","Official docs: OWASP SAML Security Cheat Sheet"],"why":"Before SAML, every web application kept its own passwords, and cross-domain single sign-on meant cookies\nthat browsers would not share between companies. Enterprises needed a way for their directory (Active\nDirectory, later Okta or Entra ID) to vouch for a user to an outside application without that application\never seeing a password. SAML, standardized in its 2.0 form in 2005, solved it with signed XML statements\npassed through the browser, so the IdP and the service provider (SP) never need a direct network\nconnection, which suited firewalled corporate networks.\n\nThe naive alternatives do not work. Sharing the customer's LDAP with the vendor exposes credentials and\nviolates every security policy. Passing a username in a URL parameter is trivially forged. Even \"signed\nXML\" is not enough by itself: early implementations verified that *a* signature in the document was valid\nand then read the user from a *different*, unsigned element, letting attackers impersonate anyone. SAML\nSSO is therefore both a protocol and a checklist of validations that a vendor must get exactly right.","how":"### Setup: metadata exchange\nThe SP and IdP exchange metadata once per customer. The SP publishes its entity ID (a unique URI) and\nAssertion Consumer Service (ACS) URL; the IdP publishes its entity ID, SSO URL and signing certificate.\nYou store the IdP's certificate per tenant, *because* that certificate is the only thing that makes an\nassertion trustworthy.\n\n### SP-initiated flow\n1. The user opens your app; home realm discovery finds their tenant.\n2. You redirect the browser to the IdP's SSO URL with an `AuthnRequest` (HTTP-Redirect binding: deflated,\n   base64, in the query string) containing a fresh request ID and your entity ID. Save the ID.\n3. The IdP authenticates the user (password, MFA, conditional access).\n4. The IdP returns an HTML form that auto-POSTs a base64 `SAMLResponse` to your ACS URL (HTTP-POST\n   binding, *because* assertions are too large for a URL).\n5. You validate and create a session.\n\n### What the assertion contains\n- `Issuer`: the IdP entity ID.\n- `Subject/NameID`: the user identifier, in a format such as persistent, email or unspecified.\n- `SubjectConfirmationData`: `Recipient` (your ACS URL), `NotOnOrAfter`, `InResponseTo` (your request ID).\n- `Conditions`: `NotBefore`, `NotOnOrAfter`, `AudienceRestriction` (your entity ID).\n- `AttributeStatement`: email, name, groups, roles.\n- An XML digital signature over the assertion, the response, or both.\n\n### The validation checklist\nEach check exists *because* skipping it enables a known attack:\n- Verify the signature with the tenant's stored certificate, require signing, and read every value **from\n  the element that the signature covers**; otherwise signature-wrapping attacks insert a forged unsigned\n  assertion next to a genuine signed one.\n- `Issuer` matches the tenant's IdP, preventing one customer's IdP from asserting into another's workspace.\n- `Audience` equals your entity ID, so an assertion minted for another SP cannot be replayed at you.\n- `Recipient` equals your ACS URL and the time window holds with small clock skew.\n- `InResponseTo` matches a request ID that *this browser session* started, and assertion IDs are recorded\n  until expiry to reject replays. Binding to the session, not to a global set of pending IDs, matters\n  *because* otherwise an attacker can start a login, then feed their own valid response into a victim's\n  browser and sign the victim in as the attacker (login CSRF).\n- If you accept IdP-initiated SSO (no `AuthnRequest`, so no `InResponseTo`), you lose request correlation;\n  many teams disable it or accept it only with strict replay caching.\n\n### Parsing, encryption, RelayState and logout\n- **Parse defensively.** The response is attacker-controlled bytes until the signature checks out, and it is\n  parsed *before* that check. Disable DTDs and external entity resolution, *because* otherwise an XXE payload\n  can make your server read local files or call internal URLs during parsing.\n- **Read text nodes carefully.** In 2018 several SAML libraries were found to read only the first text node\n  of an element. An attacker who legitimately held `ana@acme.com.evil.com` at the IdP could insert a comment\n  into their own signed assertion, `<NameID>ana@acme.com<!---->.evil.com</NameID>`; the signature still\n  verified (canonicalization drops comments) but the SP read the user as `ana@acme.com`. Use a library version that has fixed this,\n  and read the full canonicalized text.\n- **Encrypted assertions.** The IdP can encrypt the assertion to the SP's public key so the browser cannot read\n  the attributes. You decrypt, then verify the signature on the decrypted assertion; encryption hides data but\n  does not authenticate it, so it never replaces the signature checks.\n- **RelayState.** The opaque value the SP sends with the request and gets back with the response, usually the\n  page to return to. Treat it as untrusted and accept only relative paths or an allow-list, *because*\n  redirecting to whatever it contains turns your login into an open redirect.\n- **Logout.** SAML Single Logout exists but depends on every party's front-channel redirects succeeding, and\n  is unreliable in practice. Do not rely on it for revocation; use short sessions and SCIM deactivation.\n\n### Certificates\nIdP signing certificates expire and rotate. Support two active certificates per tenant during rotation,\n*because* a single hard-coded certificate turns a routine rotation into an outage for that customer.","example":"Post-decoding validation of a SAML response using `signxml` for the signature and `lxml` for reading. The\nkey design point is that all values are read from `signed`, the element the verifier returns, never from\nthe raw document.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone\nfrom lxml import etree\nfrom signxml import XMLVerifier\n\nNS = {\"saml\": \"urn:oasis:names:tc:SAML:2.0:assertion\"}\nSKEW = timedelta(minutes=2)\n\ndef ts(s: str) -> datetime:\n    return datetime.fromisoformat(s.replace(\"Z\", \"+00:00\"))\n\ndef consume(xml: bytes, tenant: dict, pending_ids: set, seen_ids: set) -> dict:\n    # pending_ids: request IDs started by THIS browser session; seen_ids: shared, kept until expiry\n    signed = XMLVerifier().verify(xml, x509_cert=tenant[\"idp_cert_pem\"]).signed_xml\n    a = signed if etree.QName(signed).localname == \"Assertion\" else signed.find(\"saml:Assertion\", NS)\n    if a is None:\n        raise PermissionError(\"no signed assertion\")\n    now = datetime.now(timezone.utc)\n    if a.findtext(\"saml:Issuer\", namespaces=NS) != tenant[\"idp_entity_id\"]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"wrong issuer\")\n    cond = a.find(\"saml:Conditions\", NS)\n    nb = cond.get(\"NotBefore\")                                # optional; NotOnOrAfter missing fails closed\n    if (nb and now < ts(nb) - SKEW) or now >= ts(cond.get(\"NotOnOrAfter\")) + SKEW:\n        raise PermissionError(\"outside validity window\")\n    if tenant[\"sp_entity_id\"] not in [x.text for x in cond.iterfind(\".//saml:Audience\", NS)]:\n        raise PermissionError(\"wrong audience\")\n    scd = a.find(\"saml:Subject/saml:SubjectConfirmation/saml:SubjectConfirmationData\", NS)\n    if scd.get(\"Recipient\") != tenant[\"acs_url\"] or scd.get(\"InResponseTo\") not in pending_ids:\n        raise PermissionError(\"wrong recipient or unsolicited response\")\n    if now >= ts(scd.get(\"NotOnOrAfter\")) + SKEW:             # bearer confirmation has its own expiry\n        raise PermissionError(\"subject confirmation expired\")\n    if a.get(\"ID\") in seen_ids:\n        raise PermissionError(\"replayed assertion\")\n    seen_ids.add(a.get(\"ID\")); pending_ids.discard(scd.get(\"InResponseTo\"))\n    attrs = {at.get(\"Name\"): [v.text for v in at.iterfind(\"saml:AttributeValue\", NS)]\n             for at in a.iterfind(\".//saml:Attribute\", NS)}\n    return {\"name_id\": a.findtext(\"saml:Subject/saml:NameID\", namespaces=NS), \"attrs\": attrs}\n```\n\nIn production, prefer a full SAML library per framework (it also handles encrypted assertions and\nmetadata), but this checklist is what you should be able to point to in its configuration.","tradeoffs":["SAML works with nearly every enterprise IdP and corporate policy, but XML signatures, canonicalization and certificate handling make it harder to implement safely than OIDC; prefer OIDC when the customer allows it.","Browser-mediated delivery needs no network path from the IdP to you, which suits locked-down networks, but it is only for interactive web login; API and agent calls still need OAuth tokens.","IdP-initiated SSO gives users a convenient tile in their portal, but removes `InResponseTo` correlation and widens replay and login-CSRF risk; enable it only with strict replay caching and short validity.","Persistent opaque NameIDs survive email changes, while email NameIDs are easy to map but change; the choice flips if the customer cannot emit a persistent identifier."],"pitfalls":["Reading the user from the raw document after checking that some signature verifies; the symptom is a pen tester logging in as an arbitrary user with a modified response.","Not checking audience or issuer per tenant; the symptom is an assertion from one customer's IdP being accepted for another customer's workspace.","Hard-coding one IdP certificate; the symptom is a single customer's logins failing on the day their IdP rotates its signing certificate.","Skipping replay protection; the symptom is a captured `SAMLResponse` from browser history granting a new session hours later within a lax validity window.","Parsing the response with DTDs and external entities enabled; the symptom is a pen-test finding that the ACS endpoint fetched an attacker-supplied URL or echoed local file contents in an error.","Following `RelayState` to any URL; the symptom is phishing links that pass through your genuine login page and land on an attacker's site.","Server clock drift; the symptom is intermittent \"assertion not yet valid\" errors that appear only on some application nodes."],"when":"Implement SAML when customers require it, which in practice means most larger enterprise deals; offer OIDC\nalongside for those who can use it. Never hand-roll XML signature verification; use a maintained library and\nverify its configuration against the checklist above. Measure login failures by reason (audience, signature,\nexpiry) per tenant, and alert ahead of IdP certificate expiry dates captured from metadata. In interviews,\nbeing able to walk the SP-initiated flow and explain signature wrapping and audience checks is the expected\ndepth for anyone shipping B2B software.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures the SAML library per tenant, stores certificates and entity IDs, enforces replay caches and writes tests with tampered, expired and wrong-audience responses.","architect":"Decides whether IdP-initiated SSO is allowed, how SAML coexists with OIDC, and how certificate rotation is handled without downtime across all tenants.","fde":"Exchanges metadata with the customer's IAM team, maps their attribute names to required fields, and debugs failed logins using a SAML tracer on a test account."},"check":{"q":"Your app verifies the SAML response signature and then extracts NameID with an XPath query over the\nwhole document. Why is this vulnerable, and what is the fix?","a":"XML signatures cover specific elements referenced by ID, not the whole document. An attacker can take a\ngenuine signed response, insert a second forged assertion elsewhere in the tree, and leave the signed one\nintact so verification still passes. A document-wide XPath may then read the forged NameID. The fix is to\nread every value only from the element the verifier reports as signed, require that exactly one assertion\nexists and that it is the signed one, and use a maintained library with signature wrapping defenses.\nThen still check issuer, audience, recipient, time window and InResponseTo."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the SAML HTTP-POST binding deliver the response through the user's browser?","options":["Browsers can decrypt assertions that servers cannot","The IdP and SP need no direct network connection, and assertions are too large for a redirect URL","It avoids the need to sign the assertion","POST requests cannot be replayed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Browser-mediated delivery works across firewalls because both parties talk only to the browser, and POST carries a large signed XML payload; it is still replayable, which is why replay caches exist."},{"q":"What does the AudienceRestriction check protect against?","options":["An expired IdP signing certificate","A user without MFA","An assertion issued for a different service provider being replayed at yours","Clock skew between servers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The audience names the SP the assertion was minted for; without checking it, a valid signed assertion for another application trusted by the same IdP could be accepted by yours."},{"q":"What is the main security cost of allowing IdP-initiated SSO?","options":["Users must re-enter passwords","Assertions can no longer be signed","There is no AuthnRequest, so InResponseTo cannot tie the response to a request you made","It disables attribute statements"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Unsolicited responses lose request correlation, which helps replay and login-CSRF attacks; mitigate with strict replay caching and short validity windows, or disable the flow."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/scim-provisioning","sources":["Hunt et al., 2015 — RFC 7643: SCIM Core Schema","Hunt et al., 2015 — RFC 7644: SCIM Protocol","Official docs: Okta and Microsoft Entra SCIM provisioning guides"],"why":"Just-in-time provisioning, creating an account the first time someone signs in through SSO, looks\nsufficient until you ask what happens next. A user who leaves the company never signs in again, so your app\nnever learns they are gone: their account, API tokens, scheduled agent jobs and shared documents remain. A\nuser who moves from Finance to Sales keeps Finance group membership in your app until their next login,\nand if their sessions are long-lived, longer. You also cannot assign work or share a document with someone\nwho has not logged in yet, because they do not exist in your system.\n\nFor an AI product this is sharper. Retrieval filters, agent permissions and audit trails are keyed by users\nand groups. If membership lags the customer's directory, the assistant keeps answering from documents a\nperson should no longer see. The naive fixes, a nightly CSV export from the customer or polling their\ndirectory API, are bespoke per customer and per IdP, need credentials into their directory, and still lag.\nSCIM inverts the direction: the IdP pushes changes to a standard endpoint you build once.","how":"### Resources and endpoints\nRFC 7643 defines the schema, RFC 7644 the protocol. You expose, under a per-tenant base URL:\n- `/Users` and `/Groups`: the core resources.\n- `/ServiceProviderConfig`, `/Schemas`, `/ResourceTypes`: discovery, telling the IdP which features you\n  support (PATCH, filtering, bulk, ETags).\n\nA User carries `id` (yours, server-assigned), `externalId` (the IdP's identifier), `userName`, `name`,\n`emails`, `active`, and optional enterprise-extension attributes such as `department` and `manager`.\nA Group carries `displayName` and `members`.\n\n### The operations\n- `POST /Users` creates. Return 201 with your `id`; return 409 (`scimType: uniqueness`) if `userName`\n  already exists, *because* a repeated or retried create must not produce a second account; IdPs normally\n  query by filter first and link to an existing match.\n- `GET /Users?filter=userName eq \"ana@acme.com\"` lets the IdP look up a user before creating or matching.\n  You must support at least the `eq` filter on `userName` and `externalId` that IdPs use.\n- `PATCH /Users/{id}` with a `PatchOp` body of `add`/`replace`/`remove` operations updates attributes.\n  Deprovisioning commonly arrives as `replace active=false`, not as `DELETE`, *because* customers want the\n  account recoverable and its history retained.\n- `DELETE /Users/{id}` hard-removes; some IdPs send it, often later than deactivation, if at all.\n- `PATCH /Groups/{id}` adds or removes `members`; this is how group-based permissions stay current. The\n  shapes are `{\"op\":\"add\",\"path\":\"members\",\"value\":[{\"value\":\"<user id>\"}]}` and\n  `{\"op\":\"remove\",\"path\":\"members[value eq \\\"<user id>\\\"]\"}`. Prefer these incremental operations to a\n  `replace` of the whole member list, *because* a 5,000-member group replaced on every change is a\n  5,000-element write each time, and a replace that races with another change can drop members.\n- List and filter responses are a `ListResponse` with `totalResults`, `startIndex` (1-based) and\n  `itemsPerPage`; IdPs page through `/Users` and `/Groups` during an initial sync, so pagination must work\n  before the first big customer turns provisioning on.\n\n### Authentication and tenancy\nThe IdP authenticates with a bearer token (or OAuth client credentials) that you issue per tenant. The\ntoken, not the request body, determines the tenant, *because* a SCIM endpoint that trusts a tenant field in\nthe payload lets one customer's IdP write into another's directory.\n\n### Joining SCIM identities to SSO identities\nSCIM and SSO are two channels describing the same person, and they must land on one account. Agree the join\nkey with the customer's admin when configuring provisioning: ideally the same immutable identifier the SSO\ntoken carries (for example the Entra object ID or the Okta user ID) sent as `externalId` or a mapped\nattribute, so a SCIM-created user and the first SSO login resolve to the same row. Joining on email or\n`userName` works until someone's address changes, and then SCIM updates one account while login creates\nanother, *because* the two channels no longer agree on who the person is. If JIT provisioning stays enabled\nalongside SCIM, it must look up by the same key before creating.\n\n### What deactivation must do\nSetting `active=false` should immediately revoke sessions and API tokens, pause the user's scheduled jobs\nand agents, and invalidate any permission cache that includes them. Deleting their data is a separate\nretention decision.\n\n### Robustness\nIdPs retry and may resend the same change; operations must be idempotent. Implementations also differ:\nsome send `op` values in different letter case, booleans as strings, or a `replace` with no `path` and an\nobject value. Parse leniently, log the raw request, and test against each IdP you support.","example":"A FastAPI handler for `PATCH /scim/v2/Users/{id}` that handles the common deactivation shapes and does the\nsecurity-relevant work, not just a column update.\n\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Header, HTTPException\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef as_bool(v):  # some IdPs send \"False\" as a string\n    return v if isinstance(v, bool) else str(v).lower() == \"true\"\n\n@app.patch(\"/scim/v2/Users/{user_id}\")\nasync def patch_user(user_id: str, body: dict, authorization: str = Header(...)):\n    tenant = await tenant_for_scim_token(authorization.removeprefix(\"Bearer \"))  # tenant from token\n    user = await db.get_user(tenant.id, user_id)\n    if user is None:\n        raise HTTPException(404, detail=\"User not found\")\n    if \"urn:ietf:params:scim:api:messages:2.0:PatchOp\" not in body.get(\"schemas\", []):\n        raise HTTPException(400, detail=\"Expected PatchOp\")\n    changes = {}\n    for op in body.get(\"Operations\", []):\n        kind, path, value = op[\"op\"].lower(), op.get(\"path\"), op.get(\"value\")\n        if kind == \"replace\" and path is None and isinstance(value, dict):\n            changes.update(value)                      # {\"op\":\"replace\",\"value\":{\"active\":false}}\n        elif kind in (\"replace\", \"add\") and path:\n            changes[path] = value                      # {\"op\":\"replace\",\"path\":\"active\",\"value\":false}\n    if \"active\" in changes:\n        changes[\"active\"] = as_bool(changes[\"active\"])\n        if user.active and not changes[\"active\"]:      # idempotent: only act on the transition\n            await sessions.revoke_all(tenant.id, user_id)\n            await api_tokens.revoke_all(tenant.id, user_id)\n            await agent_jobs.pause_for_user(tenant.id, user_id)\n            await acl_cache.invalidate_principal(tenant.id, user_id)\n    user = await db.update_user(tenant.id, user_id, changes)\n    await audit.log(tenant.id, \"scim.patch_user\", user_id, raw=body)\n    return to_scim_user(user)                          # full resource, 200\n```\n\nA real implementation also handles filtered paths such as `emails[type eq \"work\"].value` and group\nmembership patches; the point here is that deactivation is a security event with side effects.","tradeoffs":["SCIM gives near-real-time lifecycle sync with a standard you build once, but every IdP has quirks, so \"SCIM compliant\" still means per-IdP testing; JIT-only is acceptable for small customers without compliance requirements.","Soft deactivation preserves history and allows reactivation, but you must ensure inactive users are excluded everywhere; hard deletion is simpler to reason about but conflicts with audit retention.","Pushing group memberships enables group-based permissions in retrieval, but large groups generate many PATCH operations; syncing only groups assigned to your app keeps volume and blast radius small.","SCIM-managed attributes stay authoritative, but users can no longer edit them in your UI; the trade-off flips if customers expect self-service profile edits."],"pitfalls":["Treating `active=false` as a profile field update; the symptom is a deactivated user's API token still calling your API the next day.","Determining the tenant from the payload or URL alone; the symptom is a misconfigured IdP creating users in the wrong customer's directory.","Returning 201 for duplicate `userName` instead of 409; the symptom is duplicate accounts after the IdP retries a create.","Strict parsing of PATCH bodies; the symptom is provisioning working with one IdP and failing with another with 400 errors in the IdP's provisioning log.","Joining SCIM users to SSO logins by email; the symptom is duplicate accounts for one person after an email change, with permissions on one and history on the other.","Forgetting to propagate group removals to retrieval ACL caches; the symptom is a user who changed teams still getting answers from the old team's documents."],"when":"Implement SCIM once you sell to organizations with an IAM team, compliance requirements, or more than a few\ndozen users; many enterprise security reviews ask about it explicitly. Pair it with SSO, and make\ndeactivation revoke everything. Measure time from IdP change to effect in your app (test it end to end with a canary user), provisioning\nerror rate per tenant and IdP from your logs and the IdP's provisioning log, accounts with no SCIM link\n(created only by JIT), and the count of active users who have not been seen in the IdP feed. In interviews,\nexplaining why SSO alone does not deprovision, and what deactivation must trigger, is the core signal.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the `/Users` and `/Groups` endpoints, lenient PATCH parsing, idempotent operations and the revocation side effects of deactivation.","architect":"Decides the user lifecycle model (soft versus hard delete, retention), how SCIM identities join SSO identities, and how membership changes propagate to permission caches.","fde":"Configures the customer's Okta or Entra provisioning app, scopes which groups are pushed, and proves with a test user that removal from the IdP revokes access within minutes."},"check":{"q":"A customer's security team asks: \"If we terminate someone at 9:00, when do they lose access to your AI\nassistant and the documents it indexed?\" How do you answer, given SSO and SCIM are both in place?","a":"At 9:00 the IdP blocks new sign-ins and sends a SCIM `active=false` on its next provisioning cycle, which\ncan be near-immediate or periodic depending on IdP and configuration (Okta pushes on the change event; Entra's\nprovisioning service documents incremental cycles on the order of tens of minutes, so state the bound for\nthe customer's IdP). On receipt we revoke all sessions and API\ntokens, pause their scheduled agents and invalidate permission caches, so access ends within that delay plus\nseconds. Retrieval filters are evaluated per request against current memberships, so no indexed content is\nreachable afterwards. Their data is retained or deleted per the contract's retention policy. We would verify\nthis with a test account and show the audit log."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why do IdPs usually deprovision with PATCH active=false rather than DELETE?","options":["DELETE is not part of the SCIM protocol","Customers want the account recoverable and its history retained, so deactivation is the default","PATCH is faster to process than DELETE","DELETE would remove the user from the IdP as well"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SCIM defines DELETE, but deactivation keeps ownership, audit history and the option to reactivate; the app must still treat it as a full revocation event, not a cosmetic flag."},{"q":"How should a SCIM endpoint determine which tenant a request belongs to?","options":["From a tenant attribute in the JSON body","From the user's email domain","From the per-tenant bearer token or client credentials used to authenticate the request","From the IdP's IP address"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The credential is the trust anchor issued to one customer's IdP; payload fields and email domains can be wrong or spoofed, and IdP egress IPs are shared across many customers."},{"q":"What does SCIM provide that SSO with just-in-time provisioning does not?","options":["Multi-factor authentication","Signed login assertions","Notification of leavers and group changes even when the user never signs in again","Encryption of user passwords"],"answer":2,"explanation":"JIT only learns about users at login, so departures and moves are invisible; SCIM pushes lifecycle and membership changes from the directory as they happen."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/rbac-abac-mapping-to-customer-identity","sources":["Ferraiolo & Kuhn, 1992 — Role-Based Access Controls","NIST, 2014 — SP 800-162: Guide to Attribute Based Access Control (ABAC)","Pang et al., 2019 — Zanzibar: Google's Consistent, Global Authorization System"],"why":"A customer arrives with an Entra or Okta directory full of groups like `GRP-FIN-EMEA-RO`, `All Staff` and\n`Contractors-2023`, and attributes like `department=Legal` and `country=DE`. Your product has roles such as\n`viewer`, `editor` and `admin`, and documents synced from their systems with their own ACLs. Nobody\ndesigned these to fit.\n\nThe naive approaches fail in predictable ways. Hard-coding group names in code (\"if 'Admins' in groups\")\nworks for one customer and is wrong for the next, and breaks on rename. Granting everyone in the tenant the\nsame access ignores the fact that their source systems already restrict HR, M&A and legal content.\nRe-implementing their whole permission model inside your product creates a second source of truth that\ndrifts. Pure RBAC also struggles with rules like \"regional managers see deals in their region\": with six\nfunctions, five regions and three seniority levels, encoding each combination as a role gives 90 roles,\nand adding one region adds 18 more, each of which a customer admin must bind and review. ABAC states the\nrule once, `subject.region == resource.region`, and adding a region adds no policy. And AI adds a new principal: an agent acting for a user\nmust not exceed that user's rights, nor be granted broader rights because it runs as a service. A mapping\nlayer gives each customer a reviewable, testable statement of who can do what.","how":"### Three kinds of permission inputs\n1. **Product permissions**: what actions exist in your app (configure workspace, run agent X, export).\n   These are your roles; RBAC fits *because* the action set is small and stable.\n2. **Data permissions**: who may see each document or record. For synced content these come from the\n   source system's ACLs (users and groups), so you carry them rather than invent them.\n3. **Contextual rules**: attribute conditions such as region, department, data classification, device or\n   time. ABAC fits *because* these are combinations that would otherwise multiply roles.\n\n### The mapping record (per tenant)\n- `role_bindings`: IdP group *object ID* to product role, e.g. Entra group `3f2a…` to `workspace_admin`.\n  IDs, *because* display names are neither unique nor stable.\n- `attribute_map`: IdP claim name to your attribute, e.g. `extension_region` to `region`, with allowed values.\n- `policies`: ABAC rules over subject, resource and action attributes, e.g. `resource.classification ==\n  \"confidential\" requires subject.clearance >= 2`.\n- `default`: what an authenticated user with no matching binding gets, usually nothing beyond basic access.\n\n### Translating source ACLs into the customer's identities\nCarried ACLs name principals in each source's vocabulary (SharePoint site groups, Confluence groups, Slack\nchannels, Salesforce public groups), not the IdP groups your role bindings use. There are two ways to join\nthem, and the choice is a real design decision:\n- **Expand at index time.** Resolve each document's ACL to user IDs and store those. Queries are a simple\n  membership test, but one person joining a 10,000-member group rewrites the ACL of every document that group\n  can see, so membership churn becomes index churn.\n- **Resolve at query time.** Store source principal IDs on documents, and compute for each user the set of\n  source principals they hold (from a crosswalk of source identities to IdP IDs plus synced source-group\n  membership). Documents change only when their own ACL changes; the cost moves to keeping the per-user\n  principal set current and fast to look up. It is the more common choice once groups are large.\nEither way, a source principal the crosswalk cannot resolve grants nothing, *because* guessing (by display\nname, say) is how one person's access lands on another.\n\n### Resolving a principal\nAt login (OIDC/SAML) and on SCIM updates, build the principal: stable user key, group IDs (including nested\ngroups resolved by the IdP or Graph), mapped roles and attributes. Cache with a short TTL and invalidate on\nSCIM events.\n\n### Enforcing\nEvaluate in one policy decision point, *because* scattered `if` statements cannot be audited. Fix the\ncombining rules explicitly: deny by default, an explicit deny overrides any allow, and a policy whose\nattribute is missing evaluates to deny (fail closed), with the missing-attribute rate monitored so that\nfail-closed shows up as a data-quality alert rather than as silent lockouts. Options range\nfrom a small in-process function to a policy engine (OPA/Rego, Cedar) or a relationship-based system\n(Zanzibar-style, e.g. OpenFGA or SpiceDB) when permissions follow object graphs like folders and shares.\n\n### AI-specific enforcement\n- **Retrieval**: filter candidate chunks by the principal's source groups and attributes *before* ranking\n  and generation, never by asking the model to withhold.\n- **Agents**: effective permission = user permission ∩ agent's granted scope. The agent never gains\n  rights the user lacks, and a broadly entitled user does not make a narrow agent broad.\n- **Audit**: log the decision inputs (principal, rule matched) so a customer can ask \"why could Ana see this?\"","example":"An in-process evaluator showing the three layers. Tenant config is data, not code; agent scope intersects\nwith user rights.\n\n```python\nTENANT_CFG = {\n    \"acme\": {\n        \"role_bindings\": {\"3f2a9c1e\": \"workspace_admin\", \"8b7d0e44\": \"analyst\"},  # Entra group IDs\n        \"attribute_map\": {\"extension_region\": \"region\", \"department\": \"department\"},\n    }\n}\nROLE_ACTIONS = {\"workspace_admin\": {\"configure\", \"query\", \"export\"}, \"analyst\": {\"query\"}}\n\ndef principal(tenant: str, claims: dict) -> dict:\n    cfg = TENANT_CFG[tenant]\n    groups = set(claims.get(\"groups\", []))\n    roles = {r for g, r in cfg[\"role_bindings\"].items() if g in groups}\n    attrs = {ours: claims.get(theirs) for theirs, ours in cfg[\"attribute_map\"].items()}\n    return {\"tenant\": tenant, \"groups\": groups, \"roles\": roles, \"attrs\": attrs}\n\ndef can_act(p: dict, action: str, agent_scope: set | None = None) -> bool:\n    allowed = set().union(*(ROLE_ACTIONS[r] for r in p[\"roles\"])) if p[\"roles\"] else set()\n    if agent_scope is not None:\n        allowed &= agent_scope                       # agent never exceeds the user, nor vice versa\n    return action in allowed\n\ndef can_read(p: dict, doc: dict) -> bool:\n    if doc[\"tenant\"] != p[\"tenant\"]:\n        return False                                 # tenant boundary first, always\n    if not (p[\"groups\"] & set(doc[\"acl_groups\"])):   # source-system ACL (RBAC on data)\n        return False\n    if doc.get(\"region\") and doc[\"region\"] != p[\"attrs\"].get(\"region\"):\n        return False                                 # ABAC: regional restriction\n    return True\n\nana = principal(\"acme\", {\"groups\": [\"8b7d0e44\", \"g-sales-emea\"], \"extension_region\": \"EMEA\"})\ndeal = {\"tenant\": \"acme\", \"acl_groups\": [\"g-sales-emea\", \"g-sales-amer\"], \"region\": \"AMER\"}\nprint(can_act(ana, \"query\"), can_act(ana, \"export\"), can_read(ana, deal))   # True False False\nprint(can_act(ana, \"query\", agent_scope={\"export\"}))                       # False\n```\n\nAna is an analyst in the EMEA sales group. She can query but not export; she is in a group on the deal's\nACL, yet the regional attribute rule denies the AMER deal; and an agent scoped only to `export` cannot query\non her behalf.","tradeoffs":["RBAC role bindings are easy for customer admins to review, but encoding contextual rules as roles explodes their number; switch to ABAC when roles start encoding region × function × level.","ABAC expresses fine-grained rules compactly, but decisions depend on attribute quality in the customer's directory; if `department` is blank for a third of users, policies silently deny or over-grant.","Carrying source-system ACLs keeps your app consistent with the customer's existing permissions, but inherits their mistakes and lags by a sync cycle; live checks against the source are exact but slow.","A central policy engine gives auditability and consistent decisions, but adds a dependency on the request path; an in-process library is faster and is enough while rules stay simple."],"pitfalls":["Binding roles to group display names; the symptom is a customer renaming a group and all its members losing or gaining access overnight.","A permissive default for unmapped users; the symptom is a newly onboarded department seeing the whole workspace because no binding matched.","Filtering retrieval after generation or relying on the prompt to withhold; the symptom is restricted facts appearing in answers, paraphrased.","Running agents with a service identity broader than the user; the symptom is an agent completing an action the user could not perform directly in the UI.","Expanding document ACLs to individual users at index time for large groups; the symptom is index write volume and permission lag that spike whenever HR moves people between big groups.","Ignoring nested groups; the symptom is users whose access comes through a parent group seeing nothing."],"when":"Introduce an explicit mapping layer as soon as a second customer brings its own directory, and before any AI\nfeature indexes customer content. Start with role bindings by group ID plus carried source ACLs; add ABAC\nrules when customers ask for regional, classification or clearance restrictions. Measure unmapped-user\ncounts per tenant, denied-request reasons, and results from periodic access reviews where a customer admin\nchecks a sample of decisions. In interviews, explain how user rights, agent scope and document ACLs combine,\nand where the decision is enforced.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements principal resolution, the policy decision function or engine integration, and pre-retrieval filters that use source ACL groups and attributes.","architect":"Defines the product permission model, chooses RBAC, ABAC or relationship-based enforcement, and sets the rule that agent permissions are the intersection with the user's.","fde":"Runs the mapping workshop with the customer's IAM admin, records group-ID bindings and attribute sources, and validates with test users from each sensitive group."},"check":{"q":"A customer wants: admins configure the workspace, everyone else queries, and deal documents are visible\nonly within the user's region unless they are in the global deals team. How do you model this?","a":"Product permissions are RBAC: bind the customer's admin group ID to `workspace_admin`, and a default or\nall-staff group ID to `analyst`. Document visibility starts from carried source ACLs. The regional rule is\nABAC: map the IdP region attribute and tag each deal with its region, then allow read when regions match or\nthe user is in the global-deals group ID. Enforce in one policy function used by the retrieval filter and by\nagent tools, and verify attribute coverage first, because users with no region would otherwise be denied\neverything."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should role bindings reference IdP group object IDs rather than names?","options":["IDs are shorter, so tokens are smaller","Names can be duplicated and renamed, while object IDs are unique and stable","SAML cannot transmit group names","Names leak information about the customer's org chart"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A rename or a second group with the same display name would silently change who holds a role; immutable IDs keep bindings correct, with names shown only for readability."},{"q":"An agent with scope {query, summarize} runs for a user who can {query, export}. What may it do?","options":["query, summarize and export","summarize only","query only","export only"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Effective permissions are the intersection of the user's rights and the agent's scope, so only query is allowed; a union would let either party amplify the other."},{"q":"When does ABAC become preferable to adding more roles?","options":["When the product has fewer than five actions","When the customer uses SAML instead of OIDC","When access depends on combinations of attributes such as region, classification and department","When users belong to only one group each"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Expressing attribute combinations as roles multiplies them, one per combination; ABAC states the rule once over attributes, at the cost of depending on attribute quality."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/webhooks","sources":["Official docs: Slack Events API and request signing","Official docs: Microsoft Graph change notifications","Official docs: Stripe webhooks"],"why":"An AI assistant indexed over Jira, Slack or SharePoint is only as fresh as its last sync. Polling every few\nminutes for every tenant and every container multiplies API calls by tenants × containers × polls, most of\nwhich return \"nothing changed\", and still leaves minutes of staleness. Illustratively, 200 tenants with 50\nwatched containers each, polled every 5 minutes, is 200 × 50 × 288 ≈ 2.9 million calls a day, and the\naverage staleness is still about half the interval. Halving staleness doubles the calls. For agent workflows (\"when a ticket\nis assigned to the AI queue, draft a reply\") polling also adds latency the user feels.\n\nWebhooks invert the direction: the source tells you when something happens. The naive receiver, though,\ndoes the work inside the HTTP handler, trusts the body, and assumes each event arrives exactly once and in\norder. All three assumptions are false. The handler times out while calling an LLM, so the sender retries and\nyou process the event twice. Anyone who discovers the URL can post fake events, for example injecting a\nforged \"document updated\" event with attacker text into your index. Events for the same object arrive out\nof order, so an older \"updated\" overwrites a newer state. And senders drop events during outages on either\nside, or disable subscriptions that fail too often. The webhook pattern is the set of habits that make an\nunreliable push channel safe to build on.","how":"### 1. Subscribe\nRegister your URL with the source, per tenant, for specific event types. Some sources confirm ownership\nfirst: Slack sends a `url_verification` challenge you must echo; Microsoft Graph sends a `validationToken`\nthat you return as plain text, and Graph subscriptions expire, so you must renew them on a schedule.\nStore subscription IDs and expiry, *because* an expired subscription fails silently: nothing arrives.\nGraph also sends lifecycle notifications (for example that a subscription needs reauthorization, or that\nnotifications were missed); handle them, *because* \"missed\" is the sender telling you to run a catch-up sync.\n\n### 2. Verify\nReject anything not provably from the sender. Most use an HMAC of the raw body (often with a timestamp)\nunder a shared secret; some use a client-state token you chose at subscription time, or mutual TLS.\nCompare in constant time and reject old timestamps to limit replay. The details belong to the\nsignature-verification lesson; the integration rule is: verify the **raw bytes** before parsing. In a\nmulti-tenant receiver, derive the tenant from which secret verified the request (or from the subscription ID\nyou stored), never from a tenant field in the body, *because* the body is exactly what an attacker controls.\n\n### 3. Acknowledge fast, process later\nReturn 2xx within the sender's timeout (Slack, for example, expects a response within three seconds)\nafter verifying and durably enqueueing, *because* any slow work in the handler causes timeouts, retries and\nduplicates. Senders differ in what happens next: most retry with backoff a bounded number of times (Slack\nmarks retries with `X-Slack-Retry-Num` and `X-Slack-Retry-Reason` headers), and some disable a subscription\nwhose endpoint keeps failing. Either way, your failure window becomes lost events.\n\n### 4. Process idempotently\nUse the sender's event ID (or a hash of the payload) as a deduplication key in the consumer. Senders retry\non non-2xx and sometimes even after success, so duplicates are normal. A dedup table is an optimisation,\nnot the guarantee: two workers can pick up the same event concurrently and both see \"not seen yet\". The\nreal guarantee is that processing is itself idempotent (re-fetch current state, upsert by key), so a\nduplicate that slips through does no harm. For side effects that are not naturally idempotent, such as\nposting a reply, claim the event with an atomic insert-if-absent before acting.\n\n### 5. Treat payloads as hints\nMany events are \"thin\" (an ID and a type). Even fat payloads may be stale by the time you process them.\nRe-fetch the current object from the API and apply it, or compare a version or modified timestamp and\ndiscard older ones, *because* ordering is not guaranteed.\n\n### 6. Reconcile\nRun a periodic incremental sync (delta query, `updated since` watermark) that catches whatever the\nwebhooks missed. Webhooks give latency; reconciliation gives correctness. Alert when reconciliation finds\nmany changes webhooks did not deliver, since that usually means a broken subscription.","example":"A FastAPI receiver for Slack Events API requests: verify the signature over the raw body, answer the URL\nchallenge, enqueue and return immediately.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib, hmac, json, os, time\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Request, Response, HTTPException\n\napp = FastAPI()\nSECRET = os.environ[\"SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET\"].encode()\n\ndef verify(raw: bytes, ts: str, sig: str) -> None:\n    if abs(time.time() - int(ts)) > 300:                     # 5-minute replay window\n        raise HTTPException(401, detail=\"stale timestamp\")\n    base = b\"v0:\" + ts.encode() + b\":\" + raw\n    expected = \"v0=\" + hmac.new(SECRET, base, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(expected, sig):               # constant-time compare\n        raise HTTPException(401, detail=\"bad signature\")\n\n@app.post(\"/hooks/slack\")\nasync def slack_events(req: Request):\n    raw = await req.body()                                   # raw bytes, before any JSON parsing\n    verify(raw, req.headers.get(\"X-Slack-Request-Timestamp\", \"0\"),\n           req.headers.get(\"X-Slack-Signature\", \"\"))\n    body = json.loads(raw)\n    if body.get(\"type\") == \"url_verification\":\n        return {\"challenge\": body[\"challenge\"]}\n    await queue.enqueue(\"slack-events\", key=body[\"event_id\"], payload=body)  # durable, then ack\n    return Response(status_code=200)\n\nasync def consume(msg):                                      # runs in a worker, not the handler\n    if await dedup.seen(msg.key):                            # event_id seen before: retry duplicate\n        return\n    ev = msg.payload[\"event\"]\n    if ev[\"type\"] == \"message\":\n        m = ev.get(\"message\") or ev.get(\"previous_message\") or ev   # edits/deletes nest the original\n        root = m.get(\"thread_ts\") or m[\"ts\"]\n        current = await slack.conversations_replies(ev[\"channel\"], root)\n        await indexer.upsert_thread(msg.payload[\"team_id\"], ev[\"channel\"], current)  # re-fetched state\n    await dedup.mark(msg.key)                                # mark only after success, so a crash retries\n```\n\nThe consumer re-fetches the whole thread rather than trusting the single message in the event, which also\nhandles edits and out-of-order delivery for that thread. Edit and delete events (`message_changed`,\n`message_deleted` subtypes) carry the affected message nested inside the event, and the event's own `ts`\nis not a message, which is why the root timestamp is read from the nested message. A deleted thread root\nmakes the re-fetch return not-found, which the indexer must treat as a delete. The dedup key is marked only\nafter processing succeeds: marking it first would turn a worker crash into a silently dropped event.","tradeoffs":["Webhooks cut freshness lag to seconds and save API calls, but add a public endpoint, subscription lifecycle and secret management; polling wins when changes are rare or the source has no webhooks.","Fat payloads avoid an API call per event, but can be stale or out of order; thin payloads plus re-fetch cost a call each but always apply current state.","Acknowledging before processing keeps the sender happy, but makes your queue the durability boundary; if the enqueue is not durable, an acknowledged event can be lost for good.","Per-tenant subscriptions isolate failures and secrets, but multiply renewal jobs; one shared app-level subscription is simpler when the source supports routing by tenant ID."],"pitfalls":["Calling an LLM or indexer inside the handler; the symptom is sender timeouts, retries and the same document embedded or the same reply drafted twice.","Verifying the signature over re-serialized JSON instead of raw bytes; the symptom is every request failing verification, after which someone disables the check.","No reconciliation job; the symptom is a few documents permanently stale after an outage, found only when a user complains.","Letting subscriptions expire or get disabled after repeated failures; the symptom is a tenant's freshness quietly dropping to the reconciliation interval.","Processing non-idempotent side effects with only a check-then-mark dedup; the symptom is occasional double replies or double tickets under load, when two workers race on the same retried event.","Trusting payload content as instructions or index text without fetching from the source; the symptom is forged events injecting attacker-controlled text into retrieval."],"when":"Use webhooks when users or agents need changes within seconds and the source supports them; keep a\nreconciliation sync regardless. Prefer polling or delta sync alone for low-change sources, sources without\nwebhooks, or customers who will not allow inbound internet traffic (then consider a relay or a pull-based\nevent API). Measure end-to-end lag from source change to index, duplicate rate, signature failures, queue\ndepth, subscription expiry horizon, and how many changes reconciliation catches that webhooks missed.\nIn interviews, the expected answer names at-least-once delivery, idempotency, fast ack and reconciliation.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes verified, fast-ack receivers, idempotent consumers keyed by event ID, re-fetch logic and subscription renewal jobs with alerting on expiry.","architect":"Decides webhook versus polling per source, where the durable queue sits, and the reconciliation cadence that bounds worst-case staleness.","fde":"Gets inbound endpoints allowed through the customer's network policy, configures subscriptions in their tenant and proves freshness with a live edit during acceptance testing."},"check":{"q":"After a two-hour outage of your queue, a customer reports that some Jira tickets edited during the\noutage still show old content in the assistant. Webhooks are back. Why, and how do you prevent it?","a":"The sender retried for a while, then gave up, or your endpoint returned errors and those events were\nnever redelivered; webhooks are not a durable log you can replay at will. Nothing will re-send those\nchanges, so they stay stale until the item changes again. Prevention is a reconciliation job: an incremental\nquery for items updated since the last confirmed watermark, run periodically and after any incident, which\nre-fetches and upserts them. Also alert on receiver error rate and queue health so outages trigger an\nimmediate catch-up sync."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a webhook handler return 2xx quickly and process the event asynchronously?","options":["Senders reject responses that contain a body","Slow handlers hit the sender's timeout, which triggers retries and duplicate processing","Asynchronous processing makes the signature valid for longer","2xx responses guarantee exactly-once delivery"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Senders retry when they do not get a timely success; doing LLM or indexing work inline causes timeouts and duplicates. Fast ack after durable enqueue keeps delivery reliable, though still at-least-once."},{"q":"Two events for the same document arrive out of order. What handling is safest?","options":["Apply them in arrival order","Drop the second event as a duplicate","Re-fetch the current object, or compare versions and ignore older updates","Ask the sender to resend both in order"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Ordering is not guaranteed, so applying payloads as they arrive can let stale data win; fetching current state or version-checking makes the outcome independent of delivery order."},{"q":"Why keep a reconciliation sync if webhooks are working?","options":["Webhooks cannot deliver deletions","Reconciliation is required to verify signatures","Events can be lost during outages or expired subscriptions, and only a sync recovers them","Webhooks only work for the first tenant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Push delivery gives low latency but no completeness guarantee; a periodic incremental sync bounds how long a missed change can stay wrong."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/incremental-synchronization-and-cdc","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Official docs: Debezium","Official docs: PostgreSQL logical decoding and replication slots","Official docs: Microsoft Graph delta query"],"why":"A RAG index or feature store over customer data is a derived copy. The source keeps changing: tickets are\nedited, records reassigned, documents deleted for legal reasons, permissions revoked. The obvious way to\nkeep the copy current is to reload it on a schedule, and for small sources that is fine.\n\nIt stops working as volume grows. As an illustrative example, a 2-million-record source where 0.5% of\nrecords change per day has about 10,000 changes daily; a nightly full reload re-reads and potentially\nre-embeds 2 million records to apply them, about 200 times the necessary work, while hammering source APIs\nand still leaving up to 24 hours of staleness. A full reload also makes \"what changed?\" invisible, so\ndownstream consumers cannot react to specific changes. The first improvement, querying rows with\n`updated_at > last_run`, is where subtle bugs live: hard-deleted rows never match the query, rows written in\ntransactions that commit after your run but carry an earlier timestamp are skipped forever, rows sharing the\nboundary timestamp are dropped or duplicated, and bulk updates that bypass the application may not touch\n`updated_at` at all. Incremental sync and CDC are the techniques for getting changes, deletes included,\ncompletely and in a defined order.","how":"### The spectrum of mechanisms\n1. **Full snapshot plus diff.** Reload and compare hashes to find changes. Correct, including deletes, but\n   cost scales with total size. Fine for small or rarely changing sources.\n2. **Watermark queries.** `WHERE updated_at > :hw`. Requires a reliable modified column (database-maintained,\n   not app-maintained), an index on it, and a way to see deletes (soft-delete column or a separate tombstone\n   table). Mitigations: re-read an overlap window, *because* transactions can commit after rows with later\n   timestamps became visible; page by the tuple `(updated_at, id)`, *because* many rows can share one\n   timestamp; take the upper bound from the database clock, not the worker's.\n3. **Source-provided change APIs.** Microsoft Graph delta tokens, Salesforce Change Data Capture events\n   and `getUpdated`/`getDeleted` calls, Jira and Confluence `updated` queries with JQL/CQL. The source tracks\n   state and often reports deletions; you persist an opaque cursor per container, *because* a token is only\n   meaningful to the API that issued it. Tokens can expire, forcing a resync.\n4. **Trigger-based CDC.** Database triggers write each change to a changelog table. Captures deletes, but\n   adds write cost to every transaction on the source.\n5. **Log-based CDC.** Read the write-ahead log (PostgreSQL logical decoding via a replication slot, MySQL\n   binlog, SQL Server CDC, Oracle redo via a supported tool). Debezium is the common open-source connector,\n   publishing change events to Kafka with `before`, `after` and `op` (`c` create, `u` update, `d` delete,\n   `r` snapshot read). Every committed change appears, in commit order per table key, with no load from\n   polling queries.\n\n### What log-based CDC events actually contain\nThe log records what the database needs for replication, which is not always what a consumer expects.\nIn PostgreSQL the table's `REPLICA IDENTITY` decides the `before` image: with the default, an update or\ndelete carries only the primary key as `before`, so a consumer that needs the old values (to remove a\ndocument from the right shard, say) must set `REPLICA IDENTITY FULL` and pay for larger log records.\nLarge out-of-line (TOASTed) column values that an update did not touch may arrive as a placeholder rather\nthan the value, so a consumer that blindly writes `after` can overwrite a document body with that\nplaceholder. A multi-row transaction arrives as separate per-row events, so a consumer can observe the\ntransaction half-applied unless it groups by transaction ID. And schema changes flow through the same\npipeline: a renamed or retyped column changes the event shape, which is why CDC deployments usually pair\nwith a schema registry and a consumer that tolerates added fields. Delivery is at-least-once, so every\nconsumer is an idempotent upsert keyed by primary key, ideally guarded by the log position (LSN) so a\nreplayed older event cannot overwrite a newer one.\n\n### Snapshot, then stream\nEvery incremental system starts with an initial snapshot and then follows changes. The hand-off must not\nlose changes made during the snapshot: log-based tools record the log position before snapshotting and\nstream from it, so overlap is resolved by idempotent upserts. A one-shot snapshot of a large table can hold\na long-running read transaction on the source for hours; Debezium's incremental snapshots instead read the\ntable in key-ordered chunks interleaved with the stream, using watermarks in the log to resolve conflicts\nbetween a chunk and concurrent changes (the approach described in Netflix's DBLog work), so a table can be\nre-snapshotted without pausing the stream.\n\n### Applying changes downstream\nUpsert by primary key and apply deletes as deletes (remove chunks and embeddings). Make application\nidempotent, *because* every mechanism above can redeliver after a crash. For an embedding index, compare a\ncontent hash before re-embedding, *because* many updates touch only metadata, such as a status or assignee,\nnot the text; update metadata in place and re-embed only when the text changed. Permission changes must flow\nthrough the same path, or the index serves revoked content.\n\n### Operating it\nPersist cursors transactionally with the applied changes (or after them, with idempotent replays). Monitor\nlag: for watermarks, now minus high-water mark; for CDC, consumer offset behind the log head. A stalled\nPostgreSQL replication slot makes the server retain WAL, which can fill the source's disk, so slot lag is a\nproduction alert, not a dashboard curiosity. PostgreSQL 13+ can cap retained WAL with\n`max_slot_wal_keep_size`, at the price of invalidating a slot that falls too far behind, which then forces a\nre-snapshot.","example":"A watermark sync from a PostgreSQL table with soft deletes, an overlap window, keyset paging on\n`(updated_at, id)`, and hash-gated re-embedding. `conn` is a psycopg connection; `index` is your vector store.\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\nOVERLAP = timedelta(minutes=10)  # illustrative; must exceed your longest write transaction\n\ndef sync_once(conn, state: dict, index, page: int = 500) -> None:\n    hi = conn.execute(\"SELECT clock_timestamp()\").fetchone()[0]  # DB clock; now() is frozen per txn\n    cursor = (state[\"high_water\"] - OVERLAP, \"\")              # overlap; \"\" sorts first for text ids\n    while True:\n        rows = conn.execute(\n            \"\"\"SELECT id, updated_at, deleted_at, title, body, acl FROM documents\n               WHERE (updated_at, id) > (%s, %s) AND updated_at <= %s\n               ORDER BY updated_at, id LIMIT %s\"\"\",\n            (*cursor, hi, page)).fetchall()\n        for doc_id, updated_at, deleted_at, title, body, acl in rows:\n            if deleted_at is not None:\n                index.delete(doc_id)                          # soft delete makes deletes visible\n                continue\n            digest = hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()\n            if index.content_hash(doc_id) == digest:\n                index.update_metadata(doc_id, title=title, acl=acl)  # no re-embedding needed\n            else:\n                index.upsert(doc_id, title=title, body=body, acl=acl, content_hash=digest)\n        if len(rows) < page:\n            break\n        cursor = (rows[-1][1], rows[-1][0])                   # keyset: last (updated_at, id)\n    state[\"high_water\"] = hi                                  # persist only after a full pass\n```\n\n`clock_timestamp()` rather than `now()` matters if the connection is reused inside one open transaction:\n`now()` returns the transaction's start time, so the upper bound would never advance. Run it every five minutes and the illustrative 10,000 daily changes cost a few tens of thousands of row reads\n(each changed row is re-read by every run whose 10-minute overlap window covers it, about three runs here),\nplus re-embedding only the subset whose text changed; the hash gate is what keeps the overlap cheap. The\n`\"\"` starting id assumes text ids; with integer or UUID keys start from 0 or the nil UUID instead. Hard deletes that bypass `deleted_at` are still invisible here, which is\nthe argument for log-based CDC or a periodic full key comparison.","tradeoffs":["Watermark queries need no infrastructure, but depend on a trustworthy `updated_at` and soft deletes; move to log-based CDC when hard deletes, bulk updates or sub-minute freshness matter.","Log-based CDC captures every committed change with minimal query load, but adds Kafka or a similar log, connector operations, schema-change handling and slot monitoring; it rarely pays off for one small table.","Source delta APIs are the only option for SaaS systems and handle deletes for you, but tokens expire and semantics vary per vendor, so you still need a full-resync path.","A wider overlap window catches later-committing transactions, but re-reads more rows each run; it is cheap with idempotent, hash-gated upserts and expensive without them."],"pitfalls":["Using `updated_at > last_run` with the worker's clock and no overlap; the symptom is a small, steady number of records that never appear in the index.","No delete path; the symptom is the assistant citing documents that were deleted from the source weeks ago.","Re-embedding on every update; the symptom is embedding cost that tracks metadata churn, like ticket status changes, rather than content changes.","An abandoned PostgreSQL replication slot after decommissioning a consumer; the symptom is the source database's disk usage climbing until the customer's DBA pages you.","Applying Debezium `after` images blindly on a PostgreSQL source with default replica identity; the symptom is documents whose body becomes a placeholder string after an unrelated metadata update, or deletes the consumer cannot route because `before` holds only the key.","Saving the cursor before changes are applied; the symptom is changes lost permanently after a worker crash mid-batch."],"when":"Use full reloads for small sources and early pilots. Move to watermark or source delta sync once reload cost\nor staleness hurts, insisting on a delete mechanism. Choose log-based CDC when you own or can access the\ndatabase, need every change including hard deletes, or need low lag, and when someone will operate the\npipeline. Keep a periodic full reconciliation regardless. Measure sync lag, changes applied per run, re-embed\nratio (re-embeds over changes; a ratio near 1 on a metadata-heavy source means the hash gate is missing),\ndeletes applied, replication-slot retained WAL for log-based CDC, and drift found by reconciliation (keys or\nhashes that differ between source and index). In interviews, explaining why naive watermarks miss\nchanges and how CDC avoids it is the expected depth.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements cursors, overlap windows, keyset paging, idempotent upserts with hash-gated re-embedding, and delete propagation into the vector index.","architect":"Picks the mechanism per source (reload, watermark, delta API, log-based CDC), defines freshness targets and owns the snapshot-to-stream and full-resync strategies.","fde":"Negotiates with the customer's DBA for replica access or a replication slot, confirms how deletes are recorded, and agrees on monitoring so CDC never threatens their database."},"check":{"q":"A sync uses `WHERE updated_at > :last_run` every five minutes. A few records modified during busy periods\nnever appear in the index, and deleted records never disappear. Explain both and fix them.","a":"Missing records: `updated_at` is set when the write happens (with `DEFAULT now()`, at transaction start,\neven earlier), but the row becomes visible only when its transaction commits. A long transaction can commit after the sync advanced past that timestamp, so the row is\nskipped forever; worker clock skew and shared boundary timestamps make it worse. Fix with the database clock\nfor the upper bound, an overlap window longer than the longest transaction, keyset paging on `(updated_at,\nid)`, and idempotent upserts. Deleted records: hard deletes leave no row to match. Fix with soft deletes or a\ntombstone table, or move to log-based CDC, which emits delete events, plus periodic key reconciliation."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a watermark query on updated_at miss rows even with a correct clock?","options":["Indexes on timestamps are approximate","A row can carry a timestamp earlier than the watermark but commit and become visible after the sync ran","PostgreSQL does not store microseconds","Rows with NULL ids are skipped"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The timestamp reflects when the write happened, not when it committed. A late commit lands behind an already-advanced watermark, which is why overlap windows or log-based CDC are needed."},{"q":"What does log-based CDC capture that a soft-delete-free watermark query cannot?","options":["Rows inserted in the last minute","Hard deletes, as explicit delete events from the transaction log","Changes to columns with indexes","Rows larger than one page"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Deleted rows leave nothing to match a query, but the log records the delete, so CDC emits an event (op d in Debezium) that the consumer applies to the index."},{"q":"An index re-embeds a document whenever its row changes. Most changes are status updates. What fixes the cost?","options":["Switch to a larger embedding model","Sync less often so changes are batched","Compare a content hash and update metadata in place unless the text changed","Delete and re-insert every changed document"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Embeddings depend on text, so metadata-only changes should update filters like status or ACL without calling the embedding model; batching reduces frequency but not the wasted re-embeds."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/retry-deduplication-reconciliation","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns","Official docs: Stripe API (idempotent requests)"],"why":"Take a concrete integration: your assistant ingests ServiceNow incidents into a retrieval index through\nwebhooks and writes resolution notes back through the REST API. Three things go wrong in a normal week,\nnone of them your bug.\n\nFirst, calls fail transiently. The customer's instance returns 429 because your sync shares the API\nquota with their own users, a 503 during their maintenance window, or a connection reset through their\nproxy. These need a retry, and a careful one: synchronised retries from every worker become a thundering\nherd that throttles the customer's own staff, which is the fastest way to get an integration switched\noff.\n\nSecond, outcomes are ambiguous. A write times out after the customer's server committed it but before\nthe response reached you. Retrying creates a second resolution note; not retrying may lose the first.\nNo retry policy fixes this; only a key that lets the target recognise the repeat does.\n\nThird, losses are silent. A webhook delivery fails and the customer's platform exhausts its own retry\nbudget; a CDC connector is down for forty minutes; a record changes between two pages of a paginated\npull and lands on neither; a deletion produces no event at all. Your system has no record of an event\nit never received, so no amount of retrying or deduplication helps. The only way to find the gap is to\ncompare what the source has with what you have.\n\nThe naive alternatives each fail on one leg. \"Retry until success\" creates duplicates and retry storms.\n\"Trust the event feed\" accumulates drift that surfaces weeks later as a confident answer about a ticket\nthat was closed. \"Re-sync everything nightly\" is expensive, re-embeds unchanged documents, and still does\nnothing about duplicated writes. Retry, deduplication and reconciliation are three mechanisms for three\ndistinct failures, and the integration is only correct with all three.","how":"### Retry: classify, then back off\nNot every error should be retried. Retryable: 408, 429, 5xx, connection resets and timeouts (where the\nrequest may nevertheless have succeeded). Not retryable: 400 and 422 (the payload is wrong and will be\nwrong again), 401 once a token refresh has been tried, 403 (a permission the customer must grant), 404\non a record that was deleted at source. Retrying a non-retryable error wastes quota and hides a bug\nbehind a delay.\n\nFor the retryable class the delay grows exponentially with jitter and is capped, `Retry-After` is\nhonoured when the platform sends it, and both attempts and total elapsed time are bounded. Two\nenterprise-specific additions matter. A **retry budget** per integration (for example, retries may be at\nmost a small percentage of calls over a rolling window) prevents an outage at the customer from turning\nyour workers into a load generator against the instance their employees share. A **circuit breaker** per\nendpoint stops calling an instance that has been failing and probes it occasionally, *because* SaaS API\nquotas are commonly metered per customer instance per day, so a burst of failed calls during an incident\ncan lock the customer's own integrations out until the quota resets.\n\nReads can be retried freely. Writes can be retried only when the target can detect a repeat, which is\nthe next mechanism. The serving module's retry lesson covers the backoff arithmetic; this lesson is about\nwhich calls are safe to retry at all.\n\n### Deduplication: make at-least-once look like once\nWhy deduplication is unavoidable: a sender that gets no acknowledgement cannot tell \"the request was\nlost\" from \"the request was applied and the reply was lost\". It must either resend (at-least-once, with\npossible duplicates) or give up (at-most-once, with possible loss). No protocol removes that ambiguity\nover an unreliable network, so \"exactly once\" is never a property of delivery; it is an *effect* you\nbuild from at-least-once delivery plus a receiver that recognises repeats. Every reliable feed therefore\nduplicates, and the only design question is where the repeat is detected.\n\nDuplicates arrive from two directions.\n\n**Inbound events** (webhooks, CDC, polled changes) are delivered at least once by every reliable\nmechanism, and sometimes out of order. The dedupe key is either the delivery's own id or, better,\n`(tenant, record_id, source_version)`, where the version is a sequence number or the source's\nlast-modified stamp. The consumer stores the last applied version per record *in the same transaction*\nas the effect (the inbox pattern) and rejects anything whose version it has already applied or exceeded.\nKeying on the version rather than the delivery id handles both redelivery and reordering: a stale update\nthat arrives after a newer one is dropped instead of overwriting it. Keys are scoped per tenant because\ntwo customers' systems can legitimately produce the same record id.\n\nThe choice of key also decides how long the dedupe state must live. A store of seen *delivery ids* only\nworks if it remembers each id for longer than the sender's redelivery window (the source's retry\nschedule, plus any manual \"replay events\" button an admin can press), so it needs a TTL chosen from that\nwindow and grows with traffic; a redelivery after the TTL is applied twice. A *last-applied version per\nrecord* is bounded by the number of records, never expires, and rejects an arbitrarily late replay,\nwhich is another reason to prefer it whenever the source provides a version.\n\n**Outbound writes** need the target to recognise a repeat. In order of preference: an idempotency key\nheader where the API supports one (payment and messaging APIs usually do; most enterprise SaaS APIs do\nnot); an *upsert on an external id* field you control, so a repeated write with the same external id\nupdates instead of inserting; and, as the fallback, your own **operation ledger**: claim an operation id\nwith status `unknown` before calling, record the remote id after, and never issue a write the ledger does\nnot know about. The ledger has a race window (a crash between the remote commit and your record), which\nis exactly what reconciliation closes.\n\n**Content duplicates** are the third kind: the same policy document uploaded to two SharePoint sites, or\nthe same attachment on three tickets. Deduplicate by a hash of the normalised content before chunking and\nembedding, otherwise retrieval returns the same passage three times and crowds out everything else.\n\n### Reconciliation: assume the first two leak\nReconciliation is a scheduled job that compares source and target directly and repairs the difference.\nIt runs in tiers *because* full comparison is expensive: first compare *counts* per partition (records by\nday of last modification), then the *set of ids and versions* for partitions that disagree or fall inside\na recent window, then *field-level content hashes* for ids whose versions match but which are suspected\nof partial writes. The output is a diff with three classes: missing in target (fetch and apply), stale in\ntarget (version behind; re-fetch), and extra in target (deleted at source without an event; tombstone\nand remove from the index). Deletes only show up under a full-id sweep, so the job keeps a slow\nbackground sweep over everything alongside the frequent window over the last few days.\n\nRepairs go through the same idempotent apply path as live events, which makes a reconciliation run safe\nto retry and safe to overlap with the live feed. The job also closes the outbound ledger: every operation\nstill marked `unknown` is resolved by querying the target for its external id, then recorded or\nre-issued.\n\nThe numbers the job emits are the health signal of the whole integration. A healthy feed repairs a\nhandful of records per run; a rising repair count means the event path is broken (an expired webhook\nsubscription, a filter that silently excludes a record type, a stopped connector), usually well before\nany user notices. The reconciliation report is also the artefact you show the customer when they ask\nwhether your copy matches their system of record.\n\n### How the three fit\nLive path: event arrives → dedupe by `(tenant, record, version)` → apply and commit the version together.\nWrite path: claim a ledger entry → call with retry → record the result. Audit path: reconciliation\ncompares source and target on a schedule, repairs through the apply path, resolves unknown ledger\nentries, and reports drift. The first two make the system fast and mostly right; the third makes it\neventually exactly right and tells you when the first two have failed.","example":"The sketch below puts the three mechanisms around one customer integration. The stores (`versions`,\n`records`, `ledger`) stand in for tables committed in the same transaction as the effect; `client` wraps\nthe customer-side API. Each function is safe to call twice, including `reconcile` itself.\n\n```python\nimport random, time\n\nclass ApiError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, status, retry_after=None): self.status, self.retry_after = status, retry_after\n\nRETRYABLE = {408, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504}\n\ndef call_with_retry(fn, max_attempts=5, base=0.5, cap=30.0):\n    for attempt in range(1, max_attempts + 1):\n        try:\n            return fn()\n        except (ApiError, TimeoutError, ConnectionError) as e:\n            status = getattr(e, \"status\", None)         # None: timeout/reset, outcome unknown\n            if (status is not None and status not in RETRYABLE) or attempt == max_attempts:\n                raise                                   # 4xx: wrong request, do not loop on it\n            delay = min(cap, base * 2 ** (attempt - 1)) * random.uniform(0.5, 1.5)\n            time.sleep(getattr(e, \"retry_after\", None) or delay)  # honour Retry-After if sent\n\ndef apply_event(db, tenant, event):\n    \"\"\"Inbound: idempotent by (tenant, record id, source version); version committed with the write.\"\"\"\n    key = (tenant, event[\"record_id\"])\n    seen = db.versions.get(key)\n    if seen is not None and event[\"version\"] <= seen:\n        return \"duplicate-or-stale\"                     # redelivery or out-of-order: no-op\n    db.records[key] = event[\"payload\"]\n    db.versions[key] = event[\"version\"]\n    return \"applied\"\n\ndef write_with_ledger(db, client, tenant, op_id, payload):\n    \"\"\"Outbound: never issue a write the ledger does not know about.\"\"\"\n    if db.ledger.get(op_id) not in (None, \"unknown\", \"needs-replay\"):\n        return db.ledger[op_id]                         # retry of a completed op returns the saved result\n    db.ledger[op_id] = \"unknown\"                        # claimed before the call; survives a crash mid-call\n    # Re-issuing an \"unknown\" op is safe only because upsert-by-external-id turns a repeat into an update.\n    # Against an insert-only API, resolve \"unknown\" by lookup (as reconcile does) before any re-issue.\n    remote_id = call_with_retry(lambda: client.upsert(external_id=op_id, body=payload))\n    db.ledger[op_id] = remote_id\n    return remote_id\n\ndef reconcile(db, client, tenant, since):\n    \"\"\"Catch what retry and dedupe cannot: events that never arrived, writes with unknown outcomes.\"\"\"\n    source = {r[\"id\"]: r[\"version\"] for r in client.list_changed(since=since)}   # paginated pull\n    target = {rid: v for (t, rid), v in db.versions.items() if t == tenant}\n    missing = [rid for rid in source if rid not in target]\n    stale = [rid for rid, v in source.items() if rid in target and v > target[rid]]\n    for rid in missing + stale:\n        apply_event(db, tenant, client.fetch(rid))      # repairs reuse the idempotent path\n    for op_id, status in list(db.ledger.items()):\n        if status == \"unknown\":\n            db.ledger[op_id] = client.find_by_external_id(op_id) or \"needs-replay\"\n    return {\"missing\": len(missing), \"stale\": len(stale)}  # alert when this stops trending to zero\n```\n\nDeletes are not in this sketch: `list_changed` only sees records that still exist, so a separate full-id\nsweep (or the platform's deleted-records endpoint) is what produces the \"extra in target\" class.","tradeoffs":["Aggressive retries raise the chance that a transient failure heals itself, but they consume the customer's shared API quota; past a modest fraction of calls (the Google SRE book's example per-client budget is retries below 10% of requests) extra retries mostly add load to an instance that is already struggling, which is why a retry budget exists.","Version-based dedupe handles reordering and redelivery, but it needs a trustworthy version from the source; when the source only offers a timestamp with second resolution, two edits in the same second collapse and you must fall back to comparing content hashes.","Reconciliation buys certainty at the price of read load on the customer's system and a second code path to maintain; a narrow frequent window plus a slow full sweep is the usual balance, and the window widens only when measured drift shows the event path is unreliable.","An operation ledger makes outbound writes safe to retry but adds a database write before every call; for high-volume, low-value writes (metrics, heartbeats) the cost is not worth it and an occasional duplicate is accepted instead."],"pitfalls":["Acknowledging a webhook with 200 before the internal write is durable; symptom: the customer's delivery logs show success, the feed looks healthy, and reconciliation keeps finding the same class of record missing.","Deduplicating by delivery id only; symptom: a stale update that arrived late overwrites a newer one and a ticket flips from closed back to open in your index.","Retrying 4xx errors; symptom: a validation error loops through the full backoff schedule, burning quota and delaying every other call in the queue, with a real bug hidden behind what looks like a slow dependency.","Treating the reconciliation output as a log nobody reads; symptom: the repair count has climbed for weeks because a webhook subscription expired, and no alert was ever set on it.","Giving a delivery-id dedupe store a TTL shorter than the source's redelivery window; symptom: duplicate notes or double-applied updates that cluster after a source outage or an admin's bulk \"resend events\", exactly when redeliveries arrive late.","Comparing source timestamps with timestamps generated on your side (different clocks, different time zones); symptom: records are perpetually judged stale and re-fetched every run, so the repair count never reaches zero."],"when":"All three apply whenever your system holds a copy of customer data or writes into a customer system,\nwhich is almost every enterprise AI deployment: a RAG index over their documents, an agent that files\ntickets, a sync of CRM records into a feature table. Size them to the stakes. A read-only index over a\nwiki can run a modest retry policy, content-hash dedupe and a nightly reconciliation. An agent that\nwrites into a system of record needs an operation ledger, upsert-by-external-id and a reconciliation\njob that resolves every unknown outcome within hours. Measure retry rate, duplicate rate and repair\ncount per run; the last one is the one to alert on. In interviews, the expected depth is to explain why\nexactly-once delivery is impossible, why dedupe therefore has to be keyed and committed with the effect,\nand why reconciliation is still required afterwards.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the error classifier, the inbox check keyed on source version, the ledger around every outbound write, and the tiered reconciliation job with its repair metrics and alerts.","architect":"Decides which writes need a ledger and which may tolerate duplicates, sets retry budgets per customer instance, and owns the reconciliation SLA that the parity promise to the customer rests on.","fde":"Negotiates with the customer's admin for an external-id field, a changes endpoint and a workable API quota, and uses the reconciliation report as the evidence that the integration is correct."},"check":{"q":"A customer's Jira webhook feed into your RAG index has run for six months with retries and dedupe\nin place. A user reports that an issue closed three weeks ago still appears open in the assistant's\nanswers. How did this happen, and what do you change?","a":"Retries and dedupe only act on events your system saw. The close event almost certainly never\narrived or was lost after acknowledgement: Jira's delivery to your endpoint failed and its own retries\nwere exhausted during an outage, or your endpoint returned 200 before the write was durable and then\ncrashed. Nothing in your system records an event it never processed, so the index drifted silently. The\nfix is reconciliation: an hourly job that pulls issues updated in the last few days from Jira's search\nAPI, compares ids and `updated` versions against the index, and repairs through the same apply path,\nplus a slow full sweep that catches deletions and anything older. Alert on the repair count per run, and\nmove the webhook acknowledgement to after the durable write."},"quiz":[{"q":"An integration already retries failed calls and deduplicates events by record id and version. Why does it still need a reconciliation job?","options":["To re-run retries that exceeded the attempt cap","Because events that were never delivered leave no trace in the target, and only comparing against the source reveals them","To compress the dedupe store once the TTL expires","Because retries are only safe for reads, so writes must be replayed nightly"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Retry and dedupe act on events the system received. A lost webhook, a connector outage or a delete with no event produce nothing to retry or dedupe; a source-versus-target comparison is the only mechanism that can see the gap."},{"q":"A write to the customer's API timed out. Which approach is correct?","options":["Do not retry, because the write may have succeeded and a second attempt would duplicate it","Retry immediately, because timeouts almost always mean the request never arrived","Retry only through a path the target can recognise as a repeat: an idempotency key, an upsert on an external id, or a ledger entry claimed before the call","Retry after confirming by reading the record, which guarantees exactly one write"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A timeout is ambiguous, so the retry must be recognisable as a repeat by the target. A read-then-write check still has a race window between the read and the write; a ledger plus reconciliation closes it."},{"q":"Every nightly reconciliation run reports the same 40 records as stale in the target, and each run \"repairs\" them. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The customer's API is rate-limiting the reconciliation job, so it cannot read the source","The dedupe store's TTL is too short, so old events are being re-applied","The repair is not converging, for example the diff compares source versions against timestamps generated on your side, or the apply path rejects the re-fetched records","The 40 records are duplicates in the source system"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A healthy job's repair set changes between runs and trends to zero. The same set recurring means the diff is detected but the repair never makes source and target compare equal, typically because the two sides are not compared like for like; monitor the job's repair outcome, not just its diff."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/sandbox-vs-production-environments","sources":["Humble & Farley, 2010 — Continuous Delivery","Official docs: Salesforce (sandboxes)"],"why":"Software teams already separate development, staging and production. Enterprise integration adds a\nsecond axis: the customer's systems have their own environments, created by the customer on their\nschedule, under their rules. A Salesforce org has sandboxes that are copies of production taken at a\npoint in time; an SAP landscape moves configuration through development and quality systems before\nproduction; ServiceNow customers clone production into sub-production instances. Your integration has\nto bind each of your environments to one of theirs, and the differences between a sandbox and production\nare where AI integrations fail.\n\nThe failures are specific. An agent that creates cases or sends emails cannot be developed against\nproduction, because every test run would have real side effects on real customers, so you build against\nthe sandbox. But the sandbox's data is a copy from weeks ago, may be a subset, and may be masked;\nprompts, retrieval settings and extraction rules tuned on it meet a different distribution in\nproduction. Ids drift apart: some platforms keep record ids when they copy production (Salesforce full\nand partial copies do), but anything created afterwards on either side, and every record in a developer\nsandbox, gets its own id, so an id you hard-coded in a prompt or a filter may work in UAT by coincidence\nand point at nothing, or at the wrong record, in production. Integrations the customer disabled in the sandbox (outbound\nemail, callouts to other systems) silently work in production. The sandbox's API quota and performance\nare not production's.\n\nThe naive alternatives both fail. \"Test in production with a test account\" leaks test data into\nproduction records, logs and the retrieval index, and the test account's permissions never match a real\nuser's. \"Assume the sandbox is production\" ships behaviour that was never exercised on real data, real\nvolume or real permissions. The practice is to use the sandbox for what it is good at (functional\ncorrectness, side effects, permissions, schema), to use production-safe methods (read-only checks, shadow\nmode, canaries) for what only production can show, and to make cross-wiring impossible rather than\nmerely discouraged.","how":"### The environment matrix\nList your environments on one axis and the customer's on the other and fill in exactly one binding per\nrow: development → developer sandbox, staging or UAT → the customer's UAT or full-copy sandbox,\nproduction → production. No row ever points at two of theirs, and no two rows share one. A binding is a versioned configuration artefact: base URL,\nthe *name* of the credential in the secret store (never the credential itself), whether writes are\nallowed, which index namespace ingested data lands in, and which identity provider issues tokens.\nCredentials are per binding *because* a customer's sandbox typically has its own connected app, API\nclient or service account, and because a production credential that exists in a non-production secret\nstore is the cross-wiring waiting to happen.\n\n### What differs, and what to do about each\n- **Data.** Sandboxes are point-in-time copies, often partial and sometimes masked. Use them for\n  functional tests, and seed them with versioned fixtures you can re-create after every refresh. Do not\n  tune prompts or thresholds on masked data alone: a name replaced by `Contact 00123` changes what an\n  extraction prompt sees. Evaluate on a sample of real data under the customer's data agreement, or in\n  shadow mode in production.\n- **Identifiers.** Record ids, picklist ids, user ids and object ids cannot be assumed equal between a\n  sandbox and production: even where a copy preserves the ids that existed at copy time, records and\n  metadata created later on either side diverge. Reference everything by stable business keys or external ids, and resolve ids at runtime\n  through metadata or lookups, never in configuration.\n- **Configuration drift.** Custom fields, validation rules and workflows move through the customer's\n  own change process (change sets, transports, update sets), so a sandbox may be ahead of or behind\n  production. Introspect the schema at startup and fail fast on a missing field, rather than discovering\n  it as a 400 response in production.\n- **Integrations and side effects.** Outbound email, callouts to other systems and scheduled jobs are\n  commonly disabled or redirected in sandboxes. Keep an explicit list per binding, so a feature that\n  \"worked in UAT\" is not sending its first real email in production.\n- **Limits and performance.** Sandboxes often run on smaller capacity with different API quotas.\n  Throughput is verified against production-shaped limits, either on a full-copy sandbox or in a\n  controlled production canary.\n- **Identity.** Users may not exist in the sandbox, or exist with different roles. Permission-sensitive\n  behaviour (what the assistant may retrieve for whom) is verified with a seeded set of sandbox users\n  that mirrors production roles.\n\n### Guardrails against cross-wiring\nEvery outbound request carries the binding pair in its context and in its logs. The HTTP client checks\nthe destination host against the binding's allow-list and refuses anything else, so a mis-set environment\nvariable produces a loud error, not a write to production. Non-production bindings have no path to\nproduction secrets; production bindings reject sandbox hosts, so sandbox data cannot be ingested into the\nproduction index. Ingested records are tagged with the binding they came from, which is what lets you\nprove later that no sandbox fixture sits behind a production answer.\n\nThe inbound direction needs its own guard, because the customer's side can cross-wire too. A sandbox\ncloned from production inherits production's configuration, and that includes any webhook\nsubscription, outbound message or notification rule that points at *your production receiver*. After\nthe next refresh, sandbox test edits start arriving at your production ingestion endpoint looking like\nreal events. An outbound host allow-list cannot see this. The fix is to authenticate the sender per\nbinding: a separate webhook signing secret or receiver URL per customer environment, plus a check of the\nsource instance identifier that most platforms put in the payload or headers, so that events from an\nunbound instance are rejected and counted rather than indexed.\n\n### The promotion path\nYour changes (prompts, mappings, flags, connector settings) move development → staging → production as\nthe same artefact with different bindings, aligned with the customer's release calendar. Sandbox\nrefreshes are events to plan for: a refresh replaces the sandbox's data and typically invalidates\ncredentials and seeded fixtures, so the staging integration breaks on the customer's schedule unless\nre-seeding is automated. Full copies are refreshed rarely (a Salesforce full sandbox, for example, can be\nrefreshed at most once every 29 days), so a refresh is negotiated, not assumed.\n\n### What only production can show\nBehaviour on the real data distribution, real volume, real permissions and real integrations. The\nproduction-safe tools for that are read-only smoke tests after deployment, shadow mode (the system runs\non live inputs without acting), and a canary to a small user slice with rollback criteria agreed in\nadvance. The sandbox proves the integration is *correct*; these prove it is *right for this customer*.","example":"A binding per environment, resolved from a single environment variable with no default, and a guard\nthat fails loudly on any mismatch between the binding and the host it points at. `http` and `secrets`\nand `index` stand for your HTTP client, secret-store client and retrieval index.\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Binding:\n    ours: str              # dev | staging | prod\n    theirs: str            # the customer's environment name\n    base_url: str\n    secret_ref: str        # name in the secret store; the value never lives in config\n    writes_allowed: bool\n    index_namespace: str   # where ingested data lands; never shared across bindings\n\nBINDINGS = {\n    \"dev\":     Binding(\"dev\", \"dev-sandbox\", \"https://dev-sandbox.crm.acme.example\", \"crm/dev\", True, \"acme-dev\"),\n    \"staging\": Binding(\"staging\", \"uat-sandbox\", \"https://uat-sandbox.crm.acme.example\", \"crm/uat\", True, \"acme-uat\"),\n    \"prod\":    Binding(\"prod\", \"production\", \"https://crm.acme.example\", \"crm/prod\", True, \"acme-prod\"),\n}\nPROD_HOSTS = {\"crm.acme.example\"}\n\ndef binding() -> Binding:\n    b = BINDINGS[os.environ[\"APP_ENV\"]]       # KeyError if unset: there is no default, least of all prod\n    host = urlparse(b.base_url).hostname\n    if (host in PROD_HOSTS) != (b.ours == \"prod\"):\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"cross-wired: {b.ours} is bound to {host}\")\n    return b\n\ndef request(method: str, path: str, body=None):\n    b = binding()\n    if method != \"GET\" and not b.writes_allowed:\n        raise PermissionError(f\"writes are disabled in {b.ours}\")\n    return http(method, b.base_url + path, token=secrets.get(b.secret_ref), json=body,\n                headers={\"X-Env-Pair\": f\"{b.ours}->{b.theirs}\"})   # tags your logs (and theirs, if logged)\n\ndef ingest(record: dict):\n    b = binding()\n    index.upsert(namespace=b.index_namespace, doc={**record, \"env_pair\": f\"{b.ours}->{b.theirs}\"})\n```\n\nThe guard is deliberately dumb: it does not try to be clever about hostnames, it only refuses to run\nwhen the two facts it knows (which binding, which host) disagree.","tradeoffs":["A full-copy sandbox gives the most realistic testing but costs the customer money, refreshes slowly, and holds a copy of production data that now needs the same protection, which is why many customers only grant partial or developer sandboxes.","Masking sandbox data protects PII but changes text distributions; the more a feature depends on free text (extraction, summarisation), the less a masked sandbox tells you and the more you need shadow mode on real traffic.","Strict host allow-lists make cross-wiring impossible but add friction whenever the customer renames an instance or adds a sandbox; the friction is worth it wherever a write path exists.","Separate credentials per binding multiply onboarding steps with the customer's admin; the alternative, one credential reused across environments, removes the only hard boundary you have."],"pitfalls":["Hard-coding sandbox record ids in prompts, filters or fixtures; symptom: everything passes UAT, then production returns empty results or \"record not found\" on the first real request.","Defaulting the environment to production when the variable is unset; symptom: a developer's laptop or a misconfigured CI job writes into the customer's live system.","Forgetting that a sandbox refresh wipes seeded data and sandbox-only credentials; symptom: staging tests fail the morning after the customer's scheduled refresh with authentication errors that nobody's change explains.","Letting sandbox-ingested documents into the production index namespace; symptom: a production answer cites a test fixture or a months-old version of a policy.","Sharing one webhook receiver and signing secret across the customer's environments; symptom: after a sandbox refresh, records with test names or obviously fake values appear in the production index, and their source instance id is the sandbox's.","Treating \"works in sandbox\" as the release gate; symptom: the first production run hits a validation rule, an email trigger or an API limit that the sandbox did not have."],"when":"Any integration that touches a customer system with side effects or permissions needs the full\npractice: a binding per environment, hard guards and a promotion path. A read-only pilot over an\nexported snapshot can start lighter, but the moment a connector or a write path appears, build the\nmatrix. Measure the time to re-establish staging after a sandbox refresh, the number of ids or settings\nthat differ between sandbox and production (each one is a latent production failure), and the share of\nbehaviour verified only in the sandbox versus in shadow or canary. Day to day it shows up as the question\n\"which environment is this pointed at?\", which must be answerable from any log line. In interviews,\nexpect to be asked how you would prevent a development deployment from writing to a customer's\nproduction system, and why sandbox parity cannot be assumed for an AI feature.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements per-binding configuration, the host allow-list guard, environment tagging on ingested data, and automated re-seeding of fixtures after a sandbox refresh.","architect":"Owns the environment matrix, the rule that credentials never cross bindings, and the decision of what must be verified in production through shadow mode or canary rather than in sandbox.","fde":"Negotiates sandbox type, refresh schedule and credentials with the customer's admin, and explains why masked sandbox data cannot be the only evidence that the assistant works."},"check":{"q":"A customer offers only a developer sandbox with synthetic data for your ticket-triage assistant,\nwhich writes a suggested category onto each new ticket. How do you reach a confident production release?","a":"Use the sandbox for what it can prove: schema and field mappings, write permissions, validation\nrules, the full create-read-update path and failure handling, with seeded tickets covering every\ncategory and edge case. It cannot prove classification quality or behaviour on real text and volume.\nFor that, negotiate a read-only sample of real tickets under the data agreement and build an evaluation\nset with the customer's SMEs, then run in shadow mode in production: the assistant reads live tickets and\nrecords its suggested category without writing it, and you compare against what agents chose. Release\nas a canary to one queue with writes enabled and a rollback criterion agreed in advance, with hard guards\nensuring the development binding can never reach the production host."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why must record ids captured in a sandbox never be hard-coded into prompts, filters or configuration?","options":["Because sandbox ids are encrypted and cannot be read by the integration","Because ids of records and metadata created independently in each environment diverge, so a hard-coded value can resolve in UAT and point at nothing, or at the wrong record, after promotion","Because ids change every time the sandbox is queried through the API","Because production ids are longer and would overflow the prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Even where a copy preserves the ids that existed at copy time, anything created later on either side gets its own id. Anything resolved by id must be looked up at runtime through stable business keys or metadata, otherwise it can break on the first production call."},{"q":"Which measure most reliably prevents a non-production deployment from writing to a customer's production system?","options":["A code review checklist item reminding engineers to check the environment variable","Logging the target host on every request so mistakes can be found afterwards","A per-binding host allow-list enforced in the client, with production credentials absent from non-production secret stores, so a mismatch fails loudly before any call","Running all tests against the sandbox before each deployment"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Review and logging detect mistakes after the fact. A guard that refuses to run when binding and host disagree, combined with credentials that physically do not exist outside production, prevents the write from happening at all."},{"q":"A feature passed every UAT test in a full-copy sandbox, but its first production run sent several hundred emails to customers. What was most likely missed?","options":["The production API quota was higher, so more records were processed","Outbound email was disabled in the sandbox, and the per-binding list of side-effect differences was never written down or tested for","The sandbox's data was older, so fewer records matched","Production users had more permissions than sandbox users"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Sandboxes commonly disable or redirect outbound integrations. A side effect that silently does nothing in the sandbox must be listed explicitly per binding and verified in shadow or canary mode, not inferred from a green UAT run."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/tenant-specific-configuration-and-feature-flags","sources":["Hodgson, 2017 — Feature Toggles (aka Feature Flags)","Official docs: OpenFeature"],"why":"The first enterprise customer gets the product as built. The second needs answers in German, a\nretrieval source that is Confluence rather than SharePoint, and a refusal policy for HR questions. The\nthird is in a regulated industry and cannot use one of your model providers at all. The fastest way to\nship each of these is an `if customer == \"acme\":` in the code, or a branch deployed only for them. Both\nwork at first and both decay quickly.\n\nHard-coded customer checks scatter policy across the codebase, so nobody can answer \"what is different\nabout Acme?\" without grepping, and every change has to consider every customer's special case by hand.\nPer-customer branches are worse: a security fix or a model upgrade must be merged into N branches,\ntesting multiplies by N, and the branches drift until some customers run code months old. The naive\nalternative of \"one configuration for everyone\" fails the other way, because enterprise deals are often\nwon on exactly these differences.\n\nThe pattern that survives is to separate *what varies per tenant* (data, resolved at runtime) from\n*the code that reads it* (one version, deployed everywhere), and to separate *configuration* (a durable\nstatement about how this tenant works) from *feature flags* (a usually temporary decision about who sees\na code path). Doing so makes differences inspectable, testable and auditable, and lets you change a\ntenant's behaviour without a deploy, which in an AI product is frequent because prompts, models and\nthresholds change far more often than code.","how":"### What goes in tenant configuration\nTypical per-tenant settings in an AI product: allowed model providers and the default model; prompt\ntemplate version; retrieval sources and their connector settings; guardrail policy and thresholds;\nlanguage and tone; data residency region; rate limits and token budgets; enabled integrations; and\nwhich tools an agent may call. Secrets (API keys, connector credentials) are *referenced* by name from\nconfiguration and stored in a secrets manager, because configuration is read widely, logged and diffed,\nand secrets must not be.\n\n### Layered resolution\nConfiguration is resolved by merging layers from general to specific, with the most specific winning:\n\n```\nglobal defaults  <  plan tier  <  region / deployment  <  tenant  <  environment override\n```\n\nThis exists *because* most tenants differ in a handful of keys. Storing full configurations per tenant\nmeans a new default must be copied into every tenant record; storing only overrides means a new default\nreaches everyone who has not opted out. The resolved result is validated against a typed schema before\nuse, because a typo in a key or an out-of-range threshold should fail at write time in an admin tool,\nnot at 2 a.m. inside a request.\n\n### Versioning and audit\nEach change to a tenant's configuration creates a new immutable version with author, timestamp and\nreason, and requests log the configuration version they ran under. This matters more for AI than for\nordinary software: when a customer reports that answers got worse on Tuesday, the first question is what\nchanged, and a prompt version or retrieval threshold changed by an admin is as likely a cause as a deploy.\nA config change that alters model behaviour should go through the same evaluation gate as code: run the\ntenant's regression suite against the new version before activating it.\n\n### Feature flags, and their four kinds\nA flag is a runtime decision about which code path runs, evaluated against a context (tenant, user,\nenvironment, percentage bucket). Hodgson's taxonomy separates them by lifetime and owner, which is the\nuseful distinction:\n- **Release flags** hide unfinished or unproven work; short-lived, removed once the feature is on for\n  everyone.\n- **Experiment flags** split traffic to compare variants (two prompts, two rerankers); removed when the\n  experiment concludes.\n- **Ops flags / kill switches** let on-call turn off an expensive or misbehaving path (a costly model, an\n  external tool) without a deploy; long-lived by design.\n- **Permission or entitlement flags** gate features by plan or contract; effectively long-lived tenant\n  configuration and better modelled as such.\n\nTreating these the same causes trouble: release flags that are never removed become permanent\nbranching, and entitlements implemented as ad hoc flags get lost when someone cleans up \"old flags\".\n\n### Evaluation mechanics\nFlag and config lookups sit on the request path, so they are served from an in-process cache refreshed\nby polling or streaming from the flag service. During an outage the cache keeps serving last-known\nvalues; the coded default is what a process uses when it has no value at all, typically a pod that\nstarted while the service was unreachable. That default must be the conservative path (feature off, stricter guardrail) *because* an outage of the\nconfig service should degrade capability, not remove protections. Percentage rollouts hash a stable key\n(tenant ID, or user ID within a tenant) so a given user stays in the same bucket across requests, and\nsalt it with the flag name so the same users are not first in line for every rollout.\nVendor-neutral SDK interfaces such as OpenFeature let application code evaluate flags without binding\nto one flag vendor.\n\nResolve once per unit of work and pin the result. An agent run or a multi-turn conversation that\nre-reads configuration at every step can switch prompt version, model or tool list halfway through when\nan admin saves a change, producing a run that no single configuration version explains and that cannot\nbe reproduced. Snapshot the resolved config (and the flag decisions) at the start of the request, job or\nconversation, carry its version in the trace, and let changes take effect at the next unit. The cache\nitself must be keyed by tenant, *because* a cache keyed by flag name alone is a cross-tenant leak: the\nfirst tenant to resolve a key sets it for everyone.\n\n### Keeping the matrix testable\nEach independent boolean doubles the number of possible combinations, so you cannot test them all. The\npractical answer is to test the combinations tenants actually run (pull the resolved configs of live\ntenants into CI), keep the number of live flags small, and give each release flag an owner and a removal\ndate.","example":"A minimal layered resolver with validation, versioning and a conservative fallback. The schema is the\ncontract; overrides are partial dictionaries.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, replace, fields\nimport hashlib\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass TenantConfig:\n    model: str = \"default-large\"\n    prompt_version: str = \"support-v7\"\n    retrieval_top_k: int = 8\n    pii_redaction: bool = True\n    allowed_providers: tuple = (\"provider-a\", \"provider-b\")\n    new_reranker_pct: int = 0           # release flag: % of users on the new reranker\n\n    def validate(self):   # explicit raises, not assert: asserts vanish under python -O\n        if not 1 <= self.retrieval_top_k <= 50: raise ValueError(\"retrieval_top_k out of range\")\n        if not 0 <= self.new_reranker_pct <= 100: raise ValueError(\"new_reranker_pct out of range\")\n        if self.model_provider() not in self.allowed_providers:\n            raise ValueError(\"model not allowed for tenant\")\n\n    def model_provider(self):\n        return \"provider-b\" if self.model.startswith(\"b-\") else \"provider-a\"\n\nLAYERS = {  # in production: rows in a versioned config store\n    \"plan:enterprise\": {\"retrieval_top_k\": 12},\n    \"tenant:acme\": {\"prompt_version\": \"support-v7-de\", \"allowed_providers\": (\"provider-a\",)},\n    \"tenant:globex\": {\"new_reranker_pct\": 25},\n}\n\ndef resolve(tenant: str, plan: str) -> TenantConfig:\n    cfg, known = TenantConfig(), {f.name for f in fields(TenantConfig)}\n    for layer in (f\"plan:{plan}\", f\"tenant:{tenant}\"):\n        override = LAYERS.get(layer, {})\n        unknown = set(override) - known\n        if unknown: raise ValueError(f\"{layer}: unknown keys {unknown}\")   # typo caught here\n        cfg = replace(cfg, **override)\n    cfg.validate()\n    return cfg\n\ndef in_rollout(flag: str, user_id: str, pct: int) -> bool:   # stable bucket per user and flag\n    return int(hashlib.sha256(f\"{flag}:{user_id}\".encode()).hexdigest(), 16) % 100 < pct\n\ncfg = resolve(\"globex\", \"enterprise\")\nprint(cfg.retrieval_top_k, in_rollout(\"new_reranker\", \"globex:u-1842\", cfg.new_reranker_pct))\n```\n\nAcme gets a German prompt and a single allowed provider; Globex gets a quarter of users on the new\nreranker; both inherit the enterprise `top_k`. The rollout percentage sits in the config only to keep\nthe sketch short; in practice it lives in the flag service with an owner and a removal date, which is\nthe separation the section above argues for. Setting `model=\"b-large\"` for Acme would fail validation\nbefore any request runs, because the contract forbids that provider for that tenant.","tradeoffs":["Runtime configuration lets you change tenant behaviour without a deploy, but a config change can break a tenant as surely as a code change, so it needs the same review and evaluation gate once it touches prompts, models or thresholds.","Layered overrides keep tenant records small and let new defaults propagate, but they make \"what does this tenant actually run?\" a computed answer; you need a tool that shows the resolved config and which layer set each key. The same propagation cuts the other way: editing a global default is a change to every tenant that has not overridden it, so once a key affects model behaviour, a default change needs a staged rollout (a release flag over the new default) rather than a single save.","Feature flags decouple deploy from release and give on-call kill switches, but each live flag doubles the theoretical test matrix; the benefit flips to a cost once flags outlive their purpose.","A managed flag service gives targeting, audit and UI for free, but adds a dependency on the request path; for a handful of long-lived entitlements a typed config table you own is simpler.","Allowing deep per-tenant customization wins deals, but past some point you are running N products; the trade flips when customization requests start requiring code paths no other tenant uses."],"pitfalls":["Customer names in code (`if tenant == \"acme\"`); the symptom is that nobody can list a tenant's differences without grepping, and a refactor silently drops one customer's behaviour.","Release flags that are never removed; the symptom is dozens of stale flags, dead code paths that still need testing, and an incident when someone flips one nobody remembers.","A fallback default that is the permissive path; the symptom shows during a flag-service outage, when guardrails or redaction quietly switch off for every tenant.","Not logging the config version per request; the symptom is a quality regression report you cannot attribute, because a prompt version changed by an admin left no trace in the trace.","Putting secrets or connector credentials directly in tenant configuration; the symptom is API keys appearing in admin UIs, config diffs and debug logs.","Re-reading configuration at every step of a long agent run or conversation; the symptom is a trace in which the prompt version or model changes mid-run, and a bug report nobody can reproduce because no single config version produced it.","Unvalidated free-form JSON config; the symptom is a typoed key that is silently ignored, so the tenant runs the default and nobody notices until the customer does."],"when":"Introduce tenant configuration as soon as a second customer needs something the first does not, and\nbefore the first customer-specific `if` lands. Keep it typed and validated from day one; retrofitting a\nschema onto a pile of free-form overrides is painful. Use release flags for every risky AI change\n(a new model, prompt or retriever), enabling it tenant by tenant while watching each tenant's eval and\nfeedback metrics. Use kill switches for anything expensive or external. Measure the number of live\nflags, their age, and how many tenants run non-default values for each key; a key that every tenant\noverrides should become the new default. In interviews, expect to be asked how you would roll out a new\nmodel to fifty enterprise tenants without a fork per customer.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the typed resolver, caches lookups on the request path, logs config version and flag decisions in every trace, and deletes release flags when the rollout completes.","architect":"Decides what may vary per tenant and what may not, sets the layering model, and requires config changes affecting model behaviour to pass the same eval gate as code.","fde":"Translates each customer requirement into configuration rather than custom code, and pushes back when a request would need a tenant-only code path the product team must then maintain."},"check":{"q":"You must move fifty enterprise tenants from one LLM to another. Three are contractually barred\nfrom the new provider, and two have had quality complaints in the past. How do you structure the rollout?","a":"Make the model a tenant configuration key constrained by each tenant's allowed-providers list, so\nthe three barred tenants cannot be switched even by mistake: validation rejects it. Gate the switch with\na release flag targeted by tenant. Run each tenant's regression suite against the new model before\nenabling it for them, start with low-risk tenants, and leave the two sensitive ones until last, ideally\nwith shadow traffic first. Log model and config version per request so any regression is attributable,\nkeep the old model as the conservative fallback and a kill switch, and remove the flag once every\neligible tenant is migrated."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should a feature-flag client fall back to the conservative path when the flag service is unreachable?","options":["Because the conservative path is always faster to execute","Because an outage of the config service should reduce capability, not remove protections such as guardrails","Because flag services guarantee the last value is stale after a timeout","Because percentage rollouts cannot be computed without the service"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the default is permissive, a flag-service outage silently disables redaction or guardrails for every tenant. A conservative default turns the same outage into a temporary loss of new features instead."},{"q":"What is the main advantage of storing tenant overrides rather than a full configuration per tenant?","options":["Overrides are cheaper to store in the database","Overrides remove the need for schema validation","A new global default reaches every tenant that has not explicitly overridden that key","Overrides make the resolved configuration easier to see at a glance"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With full copies, every new default must be backfilled into each tenant record. Overrides keep records small and let defaults propagate, at the cost of needing a tool to show the resolved result."},{"q":"Which flag is expected to be long-lived by design?","options":["A release flag for a new reranker","An experiment flag comparing two prompts","An ops kill switch that disables an expensive external tool","A flag hiding a half-built admin page"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Kill switches exist for incidents that can happen at any time, so they stay. Release and experiment flags should be removed once the rollout or experiment ends, or they become permanent branching."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/data-onboarding","sources":[],"why":"An AI product is only as good as the data it retrieves or learns from, and every enterprise customer's\ndata is different: different systems, different field meanings, different quality, different\npermission models, different legal constraints. The model and the pipeline code are shared across\ncustomers; the data is not. That makes onboarding the per-customer cost that dominates time to value.\n\nThe naive approach is \"get a full export, load it, see what happens\". It fails in predictable ways. A\nfull export takes weeks to arrange and transfer, so the first real feedback arrives late. When it\narrives, problems are found all at once and in production-shaped volume: a third of documents are\nscanned images, the \"status\" field has forty undocumented values, timestamps are in local time with no\nzone, half the records are duplicates from a past migration. Because nobody agreed in advance what\n\"loaded correctly\" means, there is no basis for sign-off, and the customer's first view of the system is\nanswers built on bad data, which damages trust that is hard to rebuild.\n\nA second failure is treating each onboarding as a one-off. The tenth customer goes through the same\ndiscovery the first did, with findings in someone's notes rather than in checks and mappings that can be\nreused. A staged, gated, documented process fixes both: problems surface on a sample when they are\ncheap to fix, each stage has an exit criterion the customer agrees to, and what you learn becomes\nreusable mappings, checks and connector settings.","how":"This lesson orients the whole sequence; each stage has its own lesson in this module.\n\n### 1. Inventory and scope\nList the sources the use case needs (from discovery), and for each: owner, system, approximate volume,\nchange rate, sensitivity classification, and how it can be reached. Agree which subset is in scope\nfirst, usually the slice that serves the pilot use case, *because* onboarding everything before proving\nvalue multiplies cost and approval work. Confirm the legal basis: data processing agreement, residency\nconstraints, whether PII may leave the customer boundary, retention rules. Record each source's\n**permission model** too (document ACLs, sharing groups, record-level visibility rules), because an\nassistant that ingests content without its permissions will show a user what the source would have\nhidden from them, and permissions are much harder to retrofit than to ingest alongside the content from\nthe first load.\n\n### 2. Access and a representative sample\nGet read access with a dedicated, least-privilege service identity, ideally against a sandbox or replica\nfirst. Pull a sample that is *representative*, not convenient: across time periods, departments, document\ntypes and record states. The first 1,000 rows of a table are often the oldest and least like current\ndata, so stratify the sample deliberately.\n\n### 3. Profile and assess quality\nProfile the sample: null rates, cardinalities, value distributions, formats, duplicates, and for\ndocuments the mix of born-digital and scanned files, languages and sizes. The output is a written\nassessment of what is usable, what needs cleaning, and what blocks the use case, shared with the customer.\n\n### 4. Map to the canonical schema\nMap source fields and value sets to your canonical model, record each decision, and quarantine values\nyou cannot map rather than defaulting them. Unmapped values go back to the customer's data owner as\nquestions, because only they know what `STATUS=7` means.\n\n### 5. Pilot load and SME validation\nLoad the sample end to end (parse, map, chunk, embed or index) into a non-production tenant and have\nthe customer's subject-matter experts check it: are these records right, are these answers grounded in\nthe right documents, is anything missing. This is where a small golden set often starts, *because* the\nsame experts who validate the data can write the first evaluation questions. Validate permissions in the\nsame pass: log in as two or three seeded users with different roles and confirm each sees only what the\nsource shows them.\n\n### 6. Backfill, then incremental sync\nRun the full historical load in batches with throttling (the source is someone's production system),\ncheckpointing so a failure resumes rather than restarts, and reconciling counts per batch.\n\nThe order of the hand-over to incremental sync matters, because a large backfill takes days and the\nsource keeps changing while it runs. If you start the change feed only after the backfill finishes,\nevery record edited during the backfill, after its batch was read, is lost: the backfill saw the old\nversion, and the feed starts too late to see the new one. So record the change-feed position first (a\nCDC log position, or a `modified_at` watermark taken *before* the first batch), run the backfill, then\nreplay changes from that recorded position. Records touched in both arrive twice; that is safe as long as\nthe apply step is idempotent and keeps the higher source version. Keep a periodic reconciliation running\nafterwards, because the backfill is a snapshot and the change feed can still miss deletes and lost\nevents.\n\n### 7. Sign-off and handover\nExit with an agreed acceptance record: coverage (records and documents loaded versus in scope), reject\nand quarantine counts with reasons, quality findings and their owners, sync freshness, and the runbook\nfor re-onboarding a source. Encode the mappings, checks and connector config as versioned artifacts so\nthe next customer on the same system starts from them.","example":"A worked scenario. An insurer wants an assistant for claims handlers over two sources: a claims system\n(a relational database, roughly 2 million claim records) and a SharePoint document library (roughly\n300,000 files). Figures are illustrative.\n\n**Week 1, inventory:** scope is narrowed to motor claims from the last three years, which cuts the claims\ntable to about 400,000 records and the library to about 90,000 files. Legal confirms claimant PII may be\nprocessed only in the customer's cloud region, which fixes the deployment target before any code.\n\n**Week 2, sample:** a stratified sample of 2,000 claims (by year, region and status) and 1,500 documents\n(by folder and file type). Profiling shows 38% of documents are scanned PDFs with no text layer, 6% are\npassword-protected, the `loss_date` column is 11% null for one region, and `status` has 27 distinct\ncodes where the documentation lists 9.\n\n**Week 3, mapping and fixes:** 18 status codes are mapped with the claims operations lead; the remaining\n9 turn out to be legacy codes from a migration and are mapped to \"closed, legacy\". Scans are routed to\nOCR. Protected files are reported back, because only the customer can supply passwords or exclude them.\n\n**Week 4, pilot:** the sample is loaded to a pilot tenant. Three claims handlers review 50 claim\nsummaries and 40 question-answer pairs; they find that adjuster notes are attached to the wrong claim\nfor one region, traced to a join on a non-unique key. Fixed and re-run.\n\n**Weeks 5 to 6, backfill and sync:** the CDC connector is started and its log position recorded before\nthe first batch runs. The full in-scope set then loads in nightly batches, throttled to stay off peak\nhours, with per-batch count reconciliation. Over those two weeks handlers keep editing claims, so when\nthe backfill ends the CDC changes since the recorded position are applied; claims edited during the\nbackfill arrive in both paths, and the version check keeps the later edit. The sign-off record\nlists coverage of 99.1% of in-scope claim records, the password-protected files (counted on the full\nlibrary, not extrapolated from the sample's 6%) as excluded with a named owner, and a nightly\nreconciliation job.","tradeoffs":["Staged onboarding finds problems on a sample when they are cheap, but takes more calendar time before \"all the data\" is in; it pays off whenever the data is unfamiliar, and is overkill for a second tenant on an identical, already-mapped system.","Narrowing scope to the pilot slice gets to value faster, but risks a design that does not generalize; include at least one awkward source in the first slice to keep the design honest.","Customer SME validation catches meaning errors no automated check can see, but SME time is scarce and expensive; ask for short, structured reviews of specific samples rather than open-ended exploration.","Reusable onboarding artifacts (mappings, checks, connector templates) make later customers faster, but only if they are kept in version control and tested; otherwise they rot into misleading defaults."],"pitfalls":["Loading the full export before profiling a sample; the symptom is a multi-week load followed by the discovery that a key field is unusable, and a restart.","Sampling the first N rows or the most convenient folder; the symptom is a pilot that looks clean and a production load full of formats and states the sample never contained.","Defaulting unmappable values to a catch-all bucket; the symptom is answers and analytics that are quietly wrong for a whole category of records.","No agreed acceptance criteria; the symptom is a sign-off meeting that turns into a debate, with the customer judging success by individual bad answers.","Running the backfill without throttling against the customer's production database; the symptom is a call from their operations team about slow screens during business hours.","Starting the incremental feed only after the backfill completes; the symptom is a set of records whose indexed version is older than the source's, all with source edit times inside the backfill window.","Ingesting content without its permissions; the symptom is a pilot user seeing a document or record in an answer that the source system would not have shown them.","Treating onboarding as finished at backfill; the symptom is a slowly diverging index because deletes and late updates after the snapshot were never reconciled."],"when":"Run this process for every new customer data source, scaled to its novelty: an unfamiliar system or a\nregulated dataset gets every stage; a new tenant on a system you have already mapped can often reuse\nmappings and go from sample to backfill quickly, but still needs profiling, because two customers on\nthe same software use its fields differently. Measure time from access granted to first validated pilot\nload, coverage against scope, quarantine and reject rates, and the number of mapping questions\noutstanding. In interviews and customer conversations, the strong signal is describing onboarding as a\ngated sequence with exit criteria rather than as \"we import their data\".","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the sample, profile and pilot-load tooling so each stage is a command rather than a notebook, with checkpointed backfills and per-batch count reconciliation.","architect":"Defines the canonical schema and onboarding stages, decides what is reusable across customers, and sets the acceptance criteria every onboarding must meet before production.","fde":"Runs onboarding inside the customer's environment, chases access and data-owner answers, and turns each finding into an agreed decision recorded with a named owner."},"check":{"q":"A customer offers to send a full database dump \"so you have everything\" on day one. Why might you\nstill insist on a staged onboarding, and what would you do with the dump?","a":"The dump does not remove the need for profiling, mapping and SME validation; it only changes where\nthe sample comes from. Loading it all first means finding unusable fields, unmapped codes and\nduplicates at full volume, after weeks of work, with no agreed definition of correct. I would accept the\ndump if legal and residency allow it, but pull a stratified sample from it, profile and map that, run a\npilot load reviewed by their experts, and only then backfill the rest. I would also note a dump is a\nsnapshot: I would ask exactly when it was taken (ideally a log position or transaction timestamp), start\nincremental sync from that point rather than from the day we finish loading, and keep reconciliation\nrunning, or every change made in between is silently missing and the system drifts from the source."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the onboarding sample be stratified rather than the first N rows of each table?","options":["Stratified samples are smaller and faster to load","The first rows are often old or atypical, so a convenient sample can miss formats and states common in current data","Database exports always return rows in random order","Stratification is required by most data processing agreements"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Convenient samples are biased toward whatever the source returns first, often legacy records. Sampling across time, region, type and state surfaces the variety the production load will actually contain."},{"q":"What is the right handling of source values that cannot be mapped to the canonical schema during onboarding?","options":["Map them to the most common canonical value to keep coverage high","Drop the records silently so they do not pollute the index","Quarantine them and send them to the customer's data owner as questions","Let the LLM infer the meaning at query time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only the data owner knows what an undocumented code means. Defaulting or dropping makes results quietly wrong; quarantining keeps the record and makes the gap visible and assignable."},{"q":"Why does onboarding not end when the historical backfill completes?","options":["The embedding model must be retrained after each backfill","Backfills always miss a fixed percentage of records","The backfill is a snapshot, and later changes and deletes need incremental sync and reconciliation","Customers require a second full import before sign-off"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The source keeps changing after the snapshot. Without incremental sync and periodic reconciliation, the system drifts from the source and serves deleted or outdated content."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/data-ingestion-from-client-systems","sources":["Kleppmann, 2017 — Designing Data-Intensive Applications","Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"why":"Inside your own company, ingestion is an engineering problem. At a client it is also a negotiation: the\ndata sits in their CRM, ERP, ticketing system or file shares; their security team decides which network\npaths exist; their platform team decides whether you may touch a database at all; and their operations\nteam will notice if your job slows the system their staff use all day. You rarely get the interface you\nwould design. You get what they can approve, often a REST API with rate limits, a nightly CSV on SFTP, a\nread replica, or an on-premises system reachable only from an agent inside their network.\n\nThe naive approach is a script that calls the API, paginates through everything, and writes straight\ninto the index, run on a schedule. It breaks in a characteristic order. Rate limits return 429s and the\nscript dies halfway, so the next run starts over. A full re-pull each night takes longer than the night\nas volume grows. Records updated during the run are skipped or duplicated because pagination shifted\nunder it. Deleted records stay in the index forever because a full pull of \"what exists\" says nothing\nabout what stopped existing unless you diff it. And because raw responses were never kept, a parser\nbug found a month later means re-pulling everything from a source that took weeks to get access to.\n\nThe pattern below exists to make ingestion resumable, incremental, gentle on the source, and replayable\nwithout going back to the client.","how":"For the generic landing-zone and pipeline design, see the data-architecture ingestion lesson; this\nlesson covers what changes when the source belongs to a client.\n\n### 1. Choose the channel from what the client can offer\n- **Vendor API pull** (Salesforce, ServiceNow, Zendesk, Graph for SharePoint): the usual default because\n  it respects the vendor's permission model and is supported. Costs: rate limits, pagination quirks,\n  and per-object API shapes.\n- **Database read replica or read-only views:** fastest for bulk data and allows SQL filtering, but\n  couples you to an internal schema that can change without notice; ask for views as a stable contract.\n- **File drop** (SFTP, a shared object-storage bucket): common for legacy systems and for teams that\n  cannot grant live access; simple and auditable, but batch-only and prone to format drift.\n- **Events or webhooks:** low latency, but delivery is at-least-once and can be lost, so it needs a\n  periodic pull or reconciliation behind it.\n- **CDC from the database log:** captures inserts, updates and deletes in order with low source load, but\n  needs DBA cooperation and connector infrastructure, so it is usually a phase-two upgrade.\n\n### 2. Decide where the connector runs\nIf the source is only reachable inside the client network, run a small agent there that pushes outbound\nto you, *because* outbound connections usually pass security review far more easily than inbound ones;\notherwise use private connectivity (private endpoints, VPN). Credentials are a dedicated service\naccount or OAuth client with read-only scope, stored in a secrets manager and rotated.\n\n### 3. Backfill, then incremental\nA historical backfill runs once, in bounded batches, off-peak, resumable per batch. Incremental runs\nthen fetch only what changed since a **watermark**, typically a `modified_at` timestamp or a sequence\nnumber from the source.\n\n### 4. Make the watermark safe\nTimestamps lie at the boundaries: several records can share the watermark's exact timestamp, clocks\nskew, and long transactions commit with earlier timestamps. So query from `watermark - overlap`, accept\nthat some records arrive twice, and deduplicate by `(source_id, version or content_hash)`. Advance the\nwatermark only *after* the batch is durably written, so a crash re-reads rather than skips. This is the\nwhole reason ingestion can be at-least-once without being wrong.\n\n### 5. Land raw, immutably\nWrite each source record as received to a raw store, keyed by source system, object type, ID and content\nhash, before any parsing or mapping. Everything downstream (mapping, parsing, chunking, embedding) can\nbe re-run from raw without re-contacting the client, which matters because access is slow to get and\ndownstream logic will change. Two details decide whether this works. First, hash only the fields that\ncarry content: many APIs return volatile fields (a computed \"last viewed\", a signed download URL that\nexpires, a request timestamp), and hashing those makes every re-read look like a new version. Second,\nland the record's **permissions** with its content (ACLs, sharing, owning group), because retrieval\nmust enforce them at query time and a raw zone without them cannot be replayed into a permission-aware\nindex.\n\n### 6. Protect the source\nHonour rate limits and `Retry-After`, cap concurrency, schedule heavy work outside business hours,\nprefer replicas over primaries, and expose a client-side switch to pause ingestion. Being a good\nneighbour is what keeps your access approved.\n\n### 7. Catch what the channel misses\nPolling by `modified_at` does not see hard deletes and some permission changes, and it only sees what\nbumps the field you poll on: Salesforce, for example, updates `SystemModstamp` on system and automated\nchanges that leave `LastModifiedDate` untouched, so a sync keyed on the latter misses them. Use the source's\ndeleted-items endpoint or tombstones where it has one, CDC where available, and otherwise a periodic ID\nreconciliation comparing source and target key sets.","example":"An incremental pull from a paginated REST API with a safe watermark, backoff on rate limits, raw\nlanding and deduplication. `api`, `raw_store` and `state` stand in for your HTTP client, object store and\ncheckpoint table.\n\n```python\nimport time, hashlib, json\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\n\nOVERLAP = timedelta(minutes=10)\n\ndef get_with_backoff(api, path, params, max_tries=6):\n    for attempt in range(max_tries):\n        r = api.get(path, params=params, timeout=30)\n        if r.status_code == 429 or r.status_code >= 500:\n            ra = r.headers.get(\"Retry-After\", \"\")      # seconds, or an HTTP-date we ignore here\n            wait = float(ra) if ra.isdigit() else 2 ** attempt\n            time.sleep(min(wait, 120)); continue\n        r.raise_for_status()\n        return r.json()\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"gave up on {path} after {max_tries} tries\")\n\ndef sync_tickets(api, raw_store, state, tenant):\n    wm = datetime.fromisoformat(state.get(tenant, \"tickets_wm\", \"1970-01-01T00:00:00+00:00\"))\n    since, newest, cursor = (wm - OVERLAP).isoformat(), wm, None\n    while True:\n        params = {\"updated_since\": since, \"page_size\": 200, \"order\": \"updated_at\"}\n        if cursor: params[\"cursor\"] = cursor\n        page = get_with_backoff(api, \"/tickets\", params)\n        for rec in page[\"items\"]:\n            body = json.dumps(rec, sort_keys=True).encode()   # assumes rec has no volatile fields\n            key = f\"{tenant}/tickets/{rec['id']}/{hashlib.sha256(body).hexdigest()[:16]}.json\"\n            if not raw_store.exists(key):          # dedupe the overlap window\n                raw_store.put(key, body)\n            newest = max(newest, datetime.fromisoformat(rec[\"updated_at\"]))\n        cursor = page.get(\"next_cursor\")\n        if not cursor: break\n    state.set(tenant, \"tickets_wm\", newest.isoformat())  # advance only after all writes succeeded\n```\n\nThe overlap re-reads ten minutes of records each run; the content-hash key makes those re-reads no-ops,\nand an unchanged record never produces a second raw object. The new watermark is the largest\n`updated_at` *seen in the data*, never your own clock at the end of the run, because the two clocks\ndisagree and a watermark from your clock can jump past records the source stamped slightly earlier.\nThe overlap should exceed the worst clock skew plus the longest transaction you expect at the source;\nten minutes is a starting value, not a derived one. A crash mid-run leaves the watermark where\nit was, so the next run resumes. Note what this cannot see: a ticket hard-deleted in the source never\nappears in `updated_since`, which is why a separate reconciliation job compares ID sets.","tradeoffs":["API pull respects the vendor's permission model and is supported, but rate limits make large backfills slow; a read replica flips the balance for bulk history when the client's DBAs will provide one.","Polling by watermark is simple and works with almost any API, but adds latency of up to one polling interval and misses deletes; events or CDC win once freshness or delete propagation becomes a requirement.","An agent inside the client network reaches on-premises systems and passes security review more easily, but you now operate software in an environment you cannot log into freely, so upgrades, monitoring and support get harder.","Keeping an immutable raw copy makes every downstream fix replayable, but stores sensitive client data a second time; retention, encryption and deletion propagation must cover the raw zone too.","File drops are the easiest channel to get approved, but you inherit the export job's failures, such as partial files, changed column orders and missed nights, so validate each file before loading it."],"pitfalls":["Advancing the watermark before writes are durable; the symptom is a small, permanent gap of records that were fetched during a crash and never re-fetched.","Using a strict `>` comparison on a timestamp watermark with no overlap; the symptom is occasional missing records that share a timestamp with the previous run's last record.","Offset pagination over a set that changes during the run; the symptom is records skipped or seen twice when inserts shift page boundaries, fixed by cursor or keyset pagination ordered on a stable key.","Ignoring `Retry-After` and retrying immediately; the symptom is the client's API gateway throttling or blocking your service account, sometimes for the whole tenant's integrations.","Hashing the whole API response, volatile fields included, for the dedupe key; the symptom is a raw zone that grows by roughly the full overlap window on every run and \"changed\" counts that never fall, although nobody edited those records.","Writing straight to the index with no raw copy; the symptom is that a parser or mapping fix requires a full re-pull, which can take days against a rate-limited API.","Assuming polling sees deletes; the symptom is answers citing documents or records the customer deleted weeks ago, often found by the customer."],"when":"Use this pattern for any recurring pull from a system you do not own. Start by asking the client what\nthey can approve, not by proposing your ideal: an API with a service account is usually fastest to get,\na file drop is the fallback for legacy systems, and CDC is worth requesting once freshness or deletes\nmatter. Measure lag (source change to landed), records per run, retry and 429 rates, raw-versus-target\ncounts from reconciliation, and run duration against the window you have. In interviews, the tell of\nexperience is mentioning the watermark overlap, advancing the checkpoint after the write, and deletes.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes connectors with cursor pagination, backoff that honours `Retry-After`, overlap watermarks and content-hash deduplication into an immutable raw zone.","architect":"Chooses the channel per source, where connectors run relative to the client network, and how raw data is retained, encrypted and deleted across the boundary.","fde":"Negotiates service accounts, network paths and off-peak windows with the client's security and platform teams, and is the first to hear when ingestion load annoys their operations staff."},"check":{"q":"Your nightly sync uses `updated_at > last_watermark` against a client API. Users report a few\nmissing tickets each week and some deleted tickets still being cited. Diagnose both and fix them.","a":"Missing tickets come from the strict comparison at the boundary: records sharing the watermark\ntimestamp, clock skew, or late-committing transactions fall behind it and are never re-read. Fix by\nquerying from the watermark minus an overlap, deduplicating by ID plus version or content hash, and\nadvancing the watermark only after writes succeed. Deleted tickets persist because a `updated_at`\nquery cannot return records that no longer exist. Fix with the API's deleted-records endpoint if it has\none, CDC if the client can provide it, or a scheduled reconciliation that diffs source and target ID sets\nand tombstones the difference through to the index."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the watermark be advanced only after the batch has been durably written?","options":["Because APIs reject requests whose watermark is in the future","Because a crash between fetch and write would otherwise skip those records permanently","Because writing first makes the batch smaller","Because the watermark must equal the earliest record in the batch"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the checkpoint moves first and the write then fails, the next run starts after records that were never stored. Advancing after the write makes failures re-read instead of skip."},{"q":"A client's security team will not allow any inbound connection to their on-premises database. What is the usual design?","options":["Ask for a nightly full database dump emailed to you","Use the vendor's public API from your cloud regardless of the policy","Run a connector agent inside their network that makes outbound connections to your service","Wait for them to migrate the database to the cloud"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Outbound-only agents are a common, reviewable compromise: no inbound ports open, the client controls the host, and data is pushed to you over a channel their security team can monitor."},{"q":"What does a timestamp-watermark poll fundamentally fail to capture?","options":["Records created after the last run","Records whose fields were edited in the source since the last run","Records returned on the second page of results","Records that were hard-deleted in the source"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A query for recently modified records can only return records that still exist. Hard deletes need a deleted-items endpoint, CDC, or a key-set reconciliation between source and target."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/data-mapping-and-canonical-schemas","sources":["Hohpe & Woolf, 2003 — Enterprise Integration Patterns"],"why":"Suppose your AI product consumes tickets from Zendesk, ServiceNow and Jira Service Management across\ntwelve customers, and feeds a retriever, a summarizer, an analytics view and an evaluation harness. If\neach consumer reads each source's native shape, every consumer contains knowledge of every source:\nthe summarizer knows ServiceNow's `priority` is an integer from 1 (critical) to 5, derived from impact and\nurgency, while Zendesk uses strings such as `urgent` and `low`, the\nretrieval filter knows which field holds the product name in each system. Adding a source means editing\nevery consumer; changing a consumer means testing it against every source. That is the N × M problem.\n\nThe naive fix is to let the LLM cope: dump the raw JSON into the prompt and let the model figure out\nwhich field is the priority. That works in a demo and fails in production in three ways. Retrieval\nfilters, access control and analytics are not LLM calls; they need a field they can query, so they still\nneed the mapping. Model interpretation is inconsistent across records and model versions, so the same\ncode can be read two ways on two days. And the meaning of a source value is often not in the data at all:\nwhether `STATUS=7` means \"escalated\" or \"awaiting customer\" is knowledge held by the client's data owner,\nand no amount of inference recovers it reliably.\n\nThe canonical data model, a named pattern in Hohpe and Woolf's catalogue, puts that knowledge in one\nplace per source: a mapping, reviewed with the people who know the data, tested, and versioned.","how":"### Designing the canonical schema\nThe canonical schema models *your* domain, the concepts your product reasons about (case, party,\ndocument, interaction), not a union of every source's fields. Keep it small and stable, *because* each\nfield is a promise every mapping must keep. Keep its scope to your product too. The pattern has a\nwell-known failure at enterprise scale: a single canonical model for a whole company tries to make\n\"customer\" mean the same thing to sales, billing and support, needs every team's agreement to change,\nand becomes the bottleneck. Domain-driven design's answer is a model per bounded context with\ntranslation at the edges, and your product's canonical schema is exactly one such context; it does not\nneed to be the client's enterprise data model, and should not try. Typical choices:\n- Types are normalized: timestamps in UTC with zone, money as amount plus ISO currency, explicit units.\n- Enumerations are closed and documented (`status ∈ {open, pending, resolved, closed}`), *because*\n  downstream logic branches on them.\n- Every record keeps provenance: source system, source ID, source version and mapping version, so any\n  canonical value can be traced back.\n- An **extensions** bag holds source fields with no canonical home, so mapping is lossless and a field\n  can be promoted later without re-ingesting.\n- Evolution is additive and versioned: new optional fields are cheap; renames and meaning changes are a\n  new version with a migration, as with any data contract.\n\n### Layers of a mapping\nMapping is more than renaming, and the layers fail in different ways:\n1. **Structural:** field renames, flattening or nesting, splitting one field into two or joining two.\n2. **Type and format:** parse dates and numbers, normalize time zones, convert units and currencies.\n3. **Value or semantic mapping:** crosswalks from source codes to canonical enums, agreed with the data\n   owner. This is where meaning lives and where most real errors happen.\n4. **Identity:** resolve source keys to canonical IDs and link entities across sources (the same\n   customer in the CRM and in billing), which may need matching rules rather than equality.\n5. **Derivation:** computed fields such as a canonical `is_escalated` from several source flags.\n\n### Mappings as declarative, versioned config\nExpress each source's mapping as data (a field table and value crosswalks) interpreted by one engine,\nrather than as hand-written code per customer, *because* data can be reviewed by non-engineers, diffed,\nvalidated and reused for the next tenant on the same system. Tenant-specific overrides layer on top of a\nper-system base mapping, since two customers on the same CRM often customize the same fields differently.\n\n### Mapping back: write paths need the reverse direction\nAn assistant that only reads needs the inbound mapping. One that writes back (updating a\nticket's status, filing a case) needs the reverse, and the reverse is not automatic, *because* value crosswalks\nare often many-to-one. If source codes `W` and `K` both map to canonical `pending`, then canonical\n`pending` has no single source code to write. Either keep the original source value on the canonical\nrecord (provenance makes this cheap) and write it back unchanged when the meaning has not changed, or\ndefine an explicit reverse crosswalk agreed with the data owner (`pending` writes `W`), and test the\nround trip, source → canonical → source, as a fixture.\n\n### Unmapped values are errors, not defaults\nWhen a value has no crosswalk entry, the record goes to a quarantine with the reason, and the value is\nreported to the data owner. Defaulting it to `other` or the most common value makes every downstream\nresult for that category silently wrong, which is worse than a visible gap.\n\n### Testing and validating\nEach mapping has fixture tests (source record in, expected canonical record out) built from profiled\nsamples, and every mapped record is validated against the canonical schema before it is written. Track\nper-field null and quarantine rates per source after each load, because a sudden rise is usually a\nsource-side change rather than a data trend.\n\n### LLM-assisted mapping\nLLMs are useful for *proposing* mappings: given source field names, descriptions and sample values,\nthey can draft a field table and crosswalk quickly. Treat the output as a suggestion that a human\nconfirms with the data owner, then freeze it as config. The model accelerates authoring; it should not\nbe the runtime mapping.","example":"A declarative mapping for one customer's ticket export with a structural rename, type normalization, a\nvalue crosswalk, an extensions bag and quarantine on unknown values.\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom zoneinfo import ZoneInfo\n\nMAPPING = {  # versioned config for tenant \"acme\", source \"legacy_helpdesk\"\n    \"version\": \"acme-helpdesk-v3\",\n    \"fields\": {\"TicketNo\": \"source_id\", \"Subj\": \"title\", \"Opened\": \"opened_at\", \"Stat\": \"status\"},\n    \"tz\": \"Europe/Berlin\",                       # source writes naive local time\n    \"status_crosswalk\": {\"N\": \"open\", \"W\": \"pending\", \"K\": \"pending\", \"E\": \"resolved\", \"Z\": \"closed\"},\n}\n\nclass Unmapped(Exception): pass                     # caller sends the record to quarantine\n\ndef map_ticket(src: dict, m=MAPPING) -> dict:\n    out = {\"source_system\": \"legacy_helpdesk\", \"mapping_version\": m[\"version\"]}\n    out.update({m[\"fields\"][k]: v for k, v in src.items() if k in m[\"fields\"]})\n    ext = {k: v for k, v in src.items() if k not in m[\"fields\"]}   # lossless: nothing is dropped\n    missing = {\"source_id\", \"opened_at\", \"status\"} - out.keys()\n    if missing:\n        raise Unmapped(f\"missing {missing} in {src.get('TicketNo')!r}\")\n    local = datetime.strptime(out[\"opened_at\"], \"%d.%m.%Y %H:%M\").replace(tzinfo=ZoneInfo(m[\"tz\"]))\n    out[\"opened_at\"] = local.astimezone(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n    code = out[\"status\"]\n    if code not in m[\"status_crosswalk\"]:\n        raise Unmapped(f\"status={code!r} for ticket {out['source_id']}\")\n    out[\"status\"], out[\"source_status\"] = m[\"status_crosswalk\"][code], code   # keep original for write-back\n    out[\"extensions\"] = ext\n    return out\n\nprint(map_ticket({\"TicketNo\": \"T-1042\", \"Subj\": \"VPN drops\", \"Opened\": \"03.11.2025 09:15\",\n                  \"Stat\": \"W\", \"Kostenstelle\": \"4711\"}))\n# opened_at becomes 2025-11-03T08:15:00+00:00 (CET is UTC+1 in November); \"Kostenstelle\" is kept in extensions.\n```\n\nThe crosswalk maps two source codes, `W` and `K`, to one canonical `pending`; that decision came from the\nclient's helpdesk lead, not from the data. A new code `X` appearing next quarter raises `Unmapped` and\nlands in quarantine with a message the FDE can forward, instead of becoming a silently wrong status.\nKeeping `source_status` means a later write-back can restore `K` rather than guessing between `W` and\n`K`. One edge the sketch does not handle: naive local times are ambiguous for one hour each autumn (the\nclock repeats 02:00 to 03:00) and nonexistent for one hour each spring, and `replace(tzinfo=...)`\nsilently picks one reading. If ordering within that hour matters, flag those values rather than trusting\nthe default.","tradeoffs":["A canonical schema turns N × M translations into roughly N + M, but costs an upfront modelling effort and an extra hop; with one source and one consumer, mapping directly is simpler and fine.","A small canonical schema is stable and easy to map to, but pushes useful source detail into extensions; promote a field once several consumers need it, not before.","Declarative mappings are reviewable and reusable, but awkward for genuinely complex logic such as multi-record identity resolution; allow a code escape hatch for those cases, tested like any code.","Strict quarantine on unknown values keeps results correct, but lowers coverage and creates support work; it is the right default when values drive filters, routing or access, and too strict for a free-text field that is only shown to users.","LLM-proposed mappings speed authoring considerably, but a confident wrong crosswalk is hard to spot; the saving holds only if a data owner reviews every proposed value mapping."],"pitfalls":["Designing the canonical schema as the union of every source's fields; the symptom is hundreds of mostly null columns and no consumer able to tell which fields are reliably populated.","Mapping by field name alone without checking values; the symptom is two sources with a `priority` field whose scales run in opposite directions, so high-priority tickets from one source sort to the bottom.","Dropping naive timestamps into UTC without the source zone; the symptom is events shifted by hours and off-by-one-day errors around midnight and daylight-saving changes.","Defaulting unknown codes; the symptom is a canonical category that grows quietly and contains records that belong elsewhere.","No mapping version on records; the symptom is that after a crosswalk fix you cannot tell which records were mapped under the old rule and need remapping from raw.","Writing back through an inverted many-to-one crosswalk; the symptom is source records whose status changes from `K` to `W` when the assistant updates an unrelated field, and the client's reports shifting for no visible reason.","Letting the LLM interpret raw source records at query time instead of mapping; the symptom is filters and permissions that do not work and answers that vary across model versions."],"when":"Introduce a canonical schema once you have more than one source system or more than one consumer of the\nsame data, which in enterprise AI is almost immediately. Keep the schema focused on the concepts your\nproduct reasons about and agree crosswalks with the client's data owners during onboarding. Measure,\nper source and mapping version, the quarantine rate, per-field null rate and the count of outstanding\nunmapped values. In day-to-day FDE work this is where most onboarding questions come from. In\ninterviews, expect to explain why a canonical model reduces coupling and where it hurts.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the mapping engine, fixture tests and quarantine path, and stamps every canonical record with source identity and mapping version for replay.","architect":"Owns the canonical schema and its versioning policy, decides what is canonical versus an extension, and keeps the model focused on the product's domain concepts.","fde":"Sits with the client's data owners to agree crosswalks, chases unmapped values, and writes the tenant-specific mapping overrides for each customer's customizations."},"check":{"q":"A colleague proposes skipping canonical mapping for a new CRM source: \"just put the raw record in\nthe prompt, the model understands Salesforce fields.\" When is that acceptable and when is it not?","a":"It is acceptable for content the model only reads and shows, in a prototype with one source, where\ninconsistency costs little. It breaks for anything that is not an LLM call: retrieval filters, access\ncontrol, analytics and evaluation need a concrete field and value set. It also ties meaning to the\nmodel's guesses, which vary across records and model versions, and cannot know a customer's custom\npicklist meanings. With a second source or consumer, every component inherits every source's quirks.\nI would map the fields used for filtering, routing and permissions to the canonical schema, keep\neverything else in extensions, and let the LLM draft the mapping for review, not perform it at runtime."},"quiz":[{"q":"With 6 source systems and 4 consumers, roughly how many translations does a canonical schema need compared with point-to-point?","options":["24 with a canonical schema versus 10 point-to-point","About 10 with a canonical schema versus 24 point-to-point","6 in both cases","4 with a canonical schema versus 6 point-to-point"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Point-to-point needs one translation per source-consumer pair, 6 × 4 = 24. A canonical model needs one mapping in per source and one read per consumer, about 6 + 4 = 10."},{"q":"Why should every canonical record carry the mapping version it was produced with?","options":["To let the index sort records by recency","Because canonical schemas cannot be changed otherwise","So that after a mapping fix you can find and re-map exactly the affected records from raw","So that consumers can parse each version differently"],"answer":2,"explanation":"When a crosswalk is corrected, records mapped under the old rule must be reprocessed. Without a version stamp you cannot tell which ones, so you either remap everything or leave errors in place."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to keep an extensions bag on canonical records?","options":["It lets consumers ignore the canonical schema entirely","It makes mapping lossless, so a source field can be promoted later without re-ingesting from the client","It replaces the need for value crosswalks","It stores the LLM's interpretation of each field"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Source fields without a canonical home are kept rather than dropped. If a consumer later needs one, it can be promoted and backfilled from stored data instead of negotiating a new pull from the client."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/data-profiling-and-quality-assessment","sources":["Wang & Strong, 1996 — Beyond Accuracy: What Data Quality Means to Data Consumers","Official docs: Great Expectations"],"why":"A schema tells you a column is called `resolution_notes` and is a text field. It does not tell you that\n40% of rows contain \"see email\", that one region writes in French, or that rows before a 2021 migration\nare truncated at 255 characters. Documentation, when it exists, describes the system as designed, and\nthe people who know the quirks are busy. Every enterprise dataset has these surprises, and an AI system\nis unusually sensitive to them because it rarely fails loudly. A pipeline that crashes on a bad date gets\nfixed the same day. A retriever that indexes 30,000 empty notes returns them as top results, and a\nsummarizer given \"see email\" invents a resolution, and both look like model quality problems.\n\nThe naive approach is to look at a few records, decide the data \"looks fine\", and build. Eyeballing\nfinds the obvious and misses what matters, which is distributional: the 11% null rate in one region, the\nduplicate records from a merge, the share of scanned PDFs. A second naive approach is to treat quality\nas absolute (\"is this data clean?\"). Quality is fitness for a purpose: a field 30% null is irrelevant if\nthe use case never reads it and blocking if it is the retrieval filter. Wang and Strong's framing of\nquality as defined by the data's consumers is the useful one.\n\nProfiling exists to replace impressions with measurements early, when findings change the plan cheaply,\nand assessment exists to connect each measurement to a decision someone owns.","how":"### What to profile in structured data\nFor each column: type conformance, null and empty rate, distinct count and cardinality, top values and\ntheir frequencies, min and max, length distribution for text, and **format patterns**, the shape of\nvalues after replacing digits and letters with placeholders (for example `9999-99-99` versus\n`99/99/9999` for dates). Across columns and tables: candidate key uniqueness, referential integrity\n(do child rows point to existing parents), functional dependencies you rely on, exact and near\nduplicates, and freshness (how old is the newest record, how often does it change).\n\nTwo checks deserve to be called out because standard null counts miss them. **Disguised missing\nvalues** are placeholders that are not null: `N/A`, `-`, `0`, `1900-01-01`, `unknown`, \"see email\". They\nshow up as a single value with suspiciously high frequency in the top-values list, or as a date\ndistribution with a spike on one day, and the real completeness of the field is the null rate *plus*\ntheir share. A **sensitivity scan** (pattern and dictionary detection of emails, phone numbers, national\nID formats, card numbers, and names in free text) tells you which fields need redaction or tighter\nhandling before anything is indexed or sent to a model; free-text fields often hold identifiers that the\nschema never declared.\n\n### How big a sample\nA sample can only report problems it contains. If a defect affects a share *p* of records, a random\nsample of *n* contains at least one with probability `1 − (1 − p)^n`, which is about `1 − e^(−np)`. For a\n0.1% defect, 2,000 rows catch it about 86% of the time and 5,000 rows over 99% of the time. The converse,\nthe \"rule of three\": if *n* rows show zero defects, the 95% upper bound on the true rate is roughly\n`3 / n`, so a clean 5,000-row sample still allows up to about 0.06%. Apply this per slice, not overall: a\nstratum with 200 sampled rows can hide a defect rate up to about 1.5%. This is why cheap metrics run on\nthe full table and the sample is reserved for checks that need human eyes or heavy compute.\n\n### What to profile in document corpora\nFile type mix; share of PDFs with a usable text layer versus image-only scans; encrypted or corrupt\nfiles; page counts and sizes; languages; exact duplicates by content hash and near duplicates by\nshingling or MinHash (drafts, versions, forwarded copies); date range; and the share of boilerplate\n(signatures, disclaimers, templates). These determine parsing routes, chunking and cost estimates.\n\n### Slice everything\nCompute every metric per meaningful slice (source system, region, year, department, document folder),\n*because* aggregate numbers hide the problems: an overall 3% null rate can be 0% everywhere and 40% in\nthe one region the pilot serves.\n\n### Assess against quality dimensions\nTurn measurements into a judgement along standard dimensions, each tied to the use case:\n- **Completeness:** are the fields the use case reads populated?\n- **Validity:** do values conform to type, format and allowed sets?\n- **Consistency:** do related fields and systems agree (a closed ticket with no close date)?\n- **Uniqueness:** are entities represented once?\n- **Timeliness:** is data fresh enough for the decisions it supports?\n- **Accuracy:** does the data match reality? This one cannot be profiled; it needs a sample checked by\n  the client's experts against ground truth, *because* a perfectly formatted wrong value passes every\n  automated test.\n\n### From findings to decisions\nEach finding is classified as acceptable, fixable in the pipeline (normalize, deduplicate, route scans\nto OCR), fixable only at the source (the client's data owner must act), or blocking (the use case\ncannot work without it). The assessment document names an owner for each, and it is shared with the\nclient, because many fixes are theirs.\n\n### From one-off profile to continuous checks\nThe thresholds discovered at onboarding become expectations evaluated on every load: null rate of\n`loss_date` below 2%, `status` within the agreed set, row count within a band of yesterday's. Tools such\nas Great Expectations or dbt tests express these declaratively. A failed check quarantines the batch or\nalerts, *because* source-side changes (an upgrade, a new form, a renamed picklist) are a common cause of\nsudden AI quality drops.","example":"A compact profiler over a client's ticket export using pandas, sliced by region. It computes the\nmeasurements that most often change an onboarding plan.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd, re\n\ndef shape(v):  # format pattern: digits -> 9, letters -> A\n    return re.sub(r\"[A-Za-z]\", \"A\", re.sub(r\"\\d\", \"9\", str(v)))\n\ndef profile(df: pd.DataFrame, slice_col: str, key: str):\n    report = {\"rows\": len(df), \"dup_keys\": int(df[key].duplicated().sum())}\n    for col in df.columns:\n        s = df[col]\n        report[col] = {\n            \"null_pct\": round(s.isna().mean() * 100, 1),\n            \"distinct\": int(s.nunique()),\n            \"top\": s.value_counts().head(5).to_dict(),\n            \"shapes\": s.dropna().map(shape).value_counts().head(3).to_dict(),\n        }\n    report[\"null_pct_by_slice\"] = (df.isna().groupby(df[slice_col]).mean() * 100).round(1)\n    return report\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"tickets_sample.csv\", dtype=str)\nr = profile(df, slice_col=\"region\", key=\"ticket_id\")\nprint(r[\"dup_keys\"], r[\"status\"][\"distinct\"], r[\"opened_at\"][\"shapes\"])\nprint(r[\"null_pct_by_slice\"][[\"closed_at\", \"resolution_notes\"]])\n```\n\nIllustrative output on a 5,000-row stratified sample: 212 duplicate `ticket_id` values (a past\nmigration loaded one year twice); `status` has 23 distinct values against 8 documented; `opened_at` has\ntwo shapes, `9999-99-99 99:99` and `99/99/9999 99:99`, meaning one source system writes slash dates\nwhose order (US `MM/DD` or European `DD/MM`) the shape cannot reveal, so you check for values with a\nfirst field above 12 and confirm with the source owner; and `resolution_notes` is 4% null overall but 37% null in the APAC slice. The assessment then\nreads: deduplicate on key plus latest version (pipeline fix); crosswalk the 23 statuses with the support\nlead (mapping); parse dates per source once the order is confirmed (pipeline fix); and for APAC, ask whether notes live in another\nsystem, because a resolution assistant without resolutions is blocked there (source question). The\n`top` entries are also where disguised nulls show: if \"see email\" is the most common\n`resolution_notes` value, the field's effective completeness is far below what `null_pct` reports.","tradeoffs":["Profiling a stratified sample is fast and cheap, but can miss rare problems; profile the full dataset for cheap metrics (counts, nulls, key uniqueness) and use samples for expensive ones such as near duplicate detection and manual accuracy review.","Strict load-time checks stop bad batches before they reach users, but every false alarm blocks fresh data; start checks as warnings, learn their natural variance, then promote stable ones to blocking.","Fixing quality in your pipeline is fast and under your control, but hides the problem from the client and must be maintained forever; for errors that originate at entry, pushing the fix to the source costs more time now and less over the life of the deployment.","Automated metrics scale across tenants, but cannot measure accuracy; SME review of a sample is slow and expensive yet is the only way to learn whether correctly formatted data is actually right."],"pitfalls":["Reporting only aggregate metrics; the symptom is a dataset judged healthy that fails badly for the one department or region the pilot serves.","Profiling once at onboarding and never again; the symptom is a sudden quality drop months later traced to a source-side upgrade that renamed a picklist value.","Counting only true nulls; the symptom is a field reported 98% complete whose most frequent value is `N/A` or `1900-01-01`, and prompts or filters that treat the placeholder as real data.","Treating format validity as accuracy; the symptom is clean-looking data that SMEs reject because values are stale, defaulted or entered into the wrong field.","Ignoring duplicates and near duplicates in document corpora; the symptom is retrieval returning five versions of the same policy and answers citing an outdated draft.","Producing a profile report with no decisions or owners; the symptom is the same findings rediscovered in the pilot, after users have already seen the consequences.","Copying production data to a laptop to profile it; the symptom is a security finding, because profiling exposes the most sensitive columns first; profile inside the approved environment."],"when":"Profile every new source during onboarding before mapping or building, and again whenever a source\nchanges version, a new tenant joins on the same system, or AI quality drops without a model or prompt\nchange. Keep the profile tied to the use case: start from the fields and documents the product reads\nand the slices users care about. Measure per-slice null rates, quarantine counts and check failures over\ntime. In customer conversations, a short written assessment with owners is often the most valuable\nartifact of the first weeks. In interviews, distinguishing profiling (measurement) from assessment\n(fitness for a purpose) is the point to make.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes the profiling job and the load-time expectations, slices metrics by source and region, and wires failed checks to quarantine and alerts instead of silent loads.","architect":"Defines the quality dimensions and thresholds that gate production per use case, and decides which fixes belong in the pipeline versus at the client's source.","fde":"Presents the assessment to the client's data owners, gets source-side fixes assigned, and arranges the SME sample review that measures accuracy."},"check":{"q":"Your RAG assistant's answer quality for one customer fell noticeably last month. No model,\nprompt or code changed. How would you use profiling to investigate?","a":"Compare the current data profile with the onboarding baseline, per slice. Check volumes first:\ndid a sync stop, or did ingestion double-load a period. Then per-field null rates, value sets and format\nshapes on the fields retrieval and prompts use: a source upgrade may have renamed a status, moved notes\nto a new field, or switched documents to scanned attachments with no text layer. Check duplicates and\nfreshness of the newest records. Any shift localized to a slice points to a source-side change. Then I\nwould add an expectation that would have caught it, so the next one quarantines a batch instead of\nreaching users."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should profiling metrics be computed per slice (region, source, year) rather than only overall?","options":["Sliced metrics are cheaper to compute","Aggregates can hide a severe problem concentrated in one slice the use case depends on","Data quality tools require a slice column","Overall metrics are always biased upward"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A 4% overall null rate can be 0% in most regions and 37% in one; if the pilot serves that region, the aggregate leads to a wrong go decision."},{"q":"Which quality dimension cannot be established by automated profiling alone?","options":["Completeness","Validity","Uniqueness","Accuracy"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Profiling checks presence, format and consistency, but a well-formed wrong value passes all of them. Accuracy needs comparison against ground truth, usually SME review of a sample."},{"q":"A field is 30% null. What determines whether that is a problem?","options":["Whether the null rate exceeds an industry-standard threshold","Whether the column is indexed in the source database","Whether the use case reads that field, for example as a retrieval filter or prompt input","Whether the nulls are stored as empty strings or as NULL"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Quality is fitness for purpose. A sparse field the product never uses is irrelevant; the same rate on a field used for filtering or answering can block the use case."}]},{"key":"enterprise-integration/client-document-parsing","sources":["Official docs: pdfplumber","Official docs: Tesseract OCR"],"why":"Most enterprise knowledge an AI system is asked about lives in documents: contracts, claims files,\npolicies, invoices, engineering reports. In a demo the corpus is a dozen clean PDFs exported from Word.\nAt a client it is hundreds of thousands of files accumulated over years, and the share that is scanned,\nskewed, stamped, handwritten on, or exported by an old system with a broken font encoding is often large.\nHow large is unknown until it is profiled, which is why parsing is a per-client problem rather than a\nsolved library choice.\n\nThe naive approach is to run one text extractor over everything. It fails silently. A scanned PDF has\nno text layer, so the extractor returns an empty string and the document disappears from the index\nwithout an error. A born-digital PDF with a bad character map returns plausible-looking garbage that is\nembedded and retrieved. A two-column contract is read across the columns, interleaving clauses. A table\nof policy limits becomes a run of numbers no longer attached to their headers, so the model answers with\nthe wrong limit and cites a real page. The opposite naive approach, sending every page through OCR or a\nvision-language model, is slow and expensive at enterprise volume and can degrade born-digital text that\nwas already exact.\n\nThe mechanics of OCR, layout analysis and table extraction are covered in the RAG document-parsing and\nmultimodal OCR lessons. This lesson is about applying them to a specific client's corpus: triage,\nrouting, confidence, review and provenance.","how":"### 1. Profile the corpus first\nFrom a stratified sample, measure file type mix, share of PDFs with a usable text layer, encrypted or\ncorrupt files, page counts, languages, and recurring templates (the same form from the same system).\nThis sets the routing rules and the cost and time estimate for the backfill.\n\n### 2. Triage each file, ideally each page\n- **Encrypted or corrupt:** report to the client with the file list; only they can supply passwords or\n  exclude files.\n- **Born-digital with a text layer:** extract directly. This is the cheapest and most exact route,\n  *because* the characters are already in the file.\n- **Image-only pages:** route to OCR. Hybrid PDFs (a scanned contract with a digital cover page) are\n  common, so decide per page, not per file.\n- **Text layer present but unusable:** detect it with a quality check, such as the share of characters\n  that are letters, the share of tokens found in a dictionary for the expected language, or replacement\n  and private-use characters. Broken font encodings produce text that exists but means nothing; treat\n  those pages as images.\n- **Text layer that is itself old OCR:** many scanners and document-management systems save\n  \"searchable PDFs\" with an invisible OCR layer under the page image, produced by whatever engine and\n  settings were in use at the time. It passes a naive \"has text\" test, but its quality is unknown, so\n  give it the same quality check, and re-OCR when it fails.\n- **Text plus embedded images:** a born-digital page can carry a pasted scan, a screenshot of a table or\n  a stamped signature block as an image. The text check passes and the image's content is lost. Measure\n  the share of the page area covered by images (PDF libraries expose image bounding boxes) and send\n  those regions, or the whole page, to OCR when it is large.\n\n### 3. OCR with preprocessing and confidence\nRender pages at sufficient resolution (Tesseract's documentation recommends at least 300 DPI), deskew,\nand set the expected languages explicitly, *because* OCR accuracy depends heavily on image quality and on\nthe language model the engine applies. Keep word-level confidence: OCR engines report it, and the page's\nmean or low-percentile confidence is the signal for routing. Treat it as a ranking signal, not a\nprobability: an engine's confidence is not calibrated against your documents, and it can be high on a\nconfidently misread stamp or low on a perfectly read unusual font. The routing threshold is therefore\nset by plotting confidence against measured error rate on labelled pages from this client and choosing\nthe point where the error becomes unacceptable for the use case.\n\n### 4. Escalate the hard pages\nPages with low OCR confidence, complex layouts, dense tables, handwriting or stamps go to a stronger and\nmore expensive route: a layout-aware document model, a cloud document-AI service if the data may leave\nthe boundary, or a vision-language model prompted to transcribe; and, below a further threshold, to human\nreview. A tiered route spends the cost where it buys accuracy, instead of on every page.\n\n### 5. Recover structure\nKeep reading order, headings, list items and tables (cells tied to row and column headers), and drop\nrepeated headers, footers and page numbers, *because* chunking and retrieval quality depend on structure.\nRecurring client templates are worth a dedicated extraction rule or schema; one form type can be a large\nshare of a corpus.\n\n### 6. Keep provenance\nStore for each text span the source file, page number and, where available, bounding box and parse route\nand confidence. Citations then point to a page the user can open, and a later parser improvement can\ntarget exactly the pages that used the weak route.\n\n### 7. Evaluate on the client's documents\nBuild a small labelled set from their corpus, stratified by route and template, and measure text accuracy\nagainst a human transcription, table cell accuracy and, end to end, whether retrieval finds the right\npage. Character error rate is the edit distance between output and reference divided by the reference\nlength: `CER = (S + D + I) / N`, counting substituted, deleted and inserted characters against *N*\nreference characters (word error rate is the same over words). Because insertions count, CER can exceed\n100% on a page of hallucinated text. Report it per route and per template, not only averaged, and add a\nfield-level check for the values the use case depends on (amounts, dates, policy numbers), since a 1%\nCER that lands on the digits of a limit is worse than 5% spread over boilerplate. Rerun the set when you\nchange a parser or a threshold.\n\n### Boundary and sensitivity\nScanned documents frequently contain signatures, ID numbers and health or financial details. Where\nresidency or contract requires it, OCR and any vision model must run inside the client's boundary, which\nconstrains which services you can use; settle this before choosing tools.","example":"Per-page triage with pdfplumber and Tesseract, routing each page to text extraction, OCR, or escalation.\nThresholds are illustrative and should be tuned on a labelled sample.\n\n```python\nimport pdfplumber, pytesseract\n\ndef text_is_usable(text: str, min_chars=200, min_alpha=0.6):\n    if len(text) < min_chars: return False\n    alpha = sum(c.isalpha() or c.isspace() for c in text) / len(text)\n    return alpha >= min_alpha and \"�\" not in text     # replacement char hints at broken encoding\n\ndef parse_pdf(path, lang=\"eng+deu\", ocr_ok=70):\n    pages = []\n    with pdfplumber.open(path) as pdf:\n        for i, page in enumerate(pdf.pages, start=1):\n            text = page.extract_text() or \"\"\n            if text_is_usable(text):\n                pages.append({\"page\": i, \"route\": \"text_layer\", \"text\": text, \"conf\": None})\n                continue\n            img = page.to_image(resolution=300).original          # PIL image\n            data = pytesseract.image_to_data(img, lang=lang, output_type=pytesseract.Output.DICT)\n            words = [(w, float(c)) for w, c in zip(data[\"text\"], data[\"conf\"]) if w.strip() and float(c) >= 0]\n            conf = sum(c for _, c in words) / len(words) if words else 0.0\n            route = \"ocr\" if conf >= ocr_ok else \"escalate\"           # VLM / layout model / human\n            pages.append({\"page\": i, \"route\": route, \"conf\": round(conf, 1),\n                          \"text\": \" \".join(w for w, _ in words)})\n    return pages\n\nfor p in parse_pdf(\"claim_88213.pdf\"):\n    print(p[\"page\"], p[\"route\"], p[\"conf\"], len(p[\"text\"]))\n```\n\nOn a typical scanned claim file this might print a text-layer cover page, several OCR pages in the\n80s, and one handwritten page in the 40s routed to escalation. Across the backfill, the share of pages\nper route becomes a cost forecast, and the escalation queue size tells you how much review capacity the\nclient must provide. The crude usability check has its own failure mode to tune for: a born-digital rate\ntable or invoice is mostly digits and punctuation, falls under the alphabetic threshold, and gets sent to\nOCR, which can only make its exact text worse, so count digits as valid characters for such templates.\nTwo more simplifications to remove before production: joining OCR words with spaces discards the line\nand block numbers Tesseract returns, which is where reading order and table rows live, and the\n200-character floor sends short but genuine digital pages (a signature page) to OCR for no gain.","tradeoffs":["Per-page routing spends expensive OCR and vision calls only where they are needed, but adds triage logic and thresholds to maintain; for a small corpus, running the strongest route on everything can be simpler and still affordable.","Vision-language models handle messy layouts, tables and handwriting better than classic OCR in many cases, but are slower, costlier, and can produce fluent transcriptions of text that is not there; use them where classic OCR confidence is low and verify on a labelled sample.","Cloud document-AI services are accurate and low-effort, but send document images outside the client's boundary; residency or contractual limits flip the choice to a self-hosted engine inside the boundary.","Human review of low-confidence pages gives the highest accuracy, but costs client staff time; reserve it for pages that matter to the use case, such as limits, amounts and signatures, rather than every page.","Template-specific extraction rules are very accurate for recurring forms, but break when the client changes the form; detect template version and fall back to the generic route on a mismatch."],"pitfalls":["Treating empty extraction as an empty document; the symptom is a coverage gap of scanned files that never appear in retrieval, found only when a user asks about one.","Trusting any non-empty text layer; the symptom is garbled text from broken font encodings embedded and retrieved, producing confident answers grounded in nonsense.","Treating OCR confidence as an accuracy guarantee, with a threshold never checked against labelled pages; the symptom is a page that passed at high confidence with a misread amount, found by a user comparing the answer with the original scan.","Deciding route per file instead of per page; the symptom is hybrid PDFs where the digital cover page is indexed and the scanned body is lost.","OCR with the wrong language setting; the symptom is high error rates on accented and non-Latin characters, which also breaks keyword search on names and terms.","Discarding page numbers and positions; the symptom is citations that point to a whole 200-page file, so users cannot verify answers and stop trusting them.","Evaluating the parser on public sample PDFs; the symptom is a parser that looked accurate in testing and fails on the client's faxed, stamped and skewed originals."],"when":"Apply this triage whenever a client corpus is more than a handful of born-digital files, which in\nenterprise work is nearly always. Start by profiling a stratified sample to learn the route mix, then set\nthresholds on a small labelled set from the client's own documents. Measure share of pages per route,\nOCR confidence distribution, escalation queue size, parse failures, and end-to-end retrieval of the right\npage. Revisit routing when a new document source or template appears. In interviews, the strong answer\nnames silent failures (empty and garbled text layers) and per-page routing rather than a favourite parser.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements per-page triage, text-layer quality checks, OCR with confidence capture and an escalation path, and stores page and bounding-box provenance with every chunk.","architect":"Chooses the parsing tiers and where each runs relative to the client boundary, and sets the accuracy targets and review policy for each document class.","fde":"Profiles the client's actual document estate, gets passwords or exclusions for protected files, arranges reviewers for escalated pages, and builds the labelled sample with client staff."},"check":{"q":"After indexing a client's 120,000-document archive, users say the assistant \"doesn't know about\"\nmany older contracts, though they are in the source library. What is your diagnosis process?","a":"First compare per-document coverage: how many files produced zero or near-zero text, grouped by\nyear and folder. Older contracts are likely scans with no text layer, extracted as empty strings and\nsilently indexed as nothing, or hybrid PDFs where only a digital cover page was read. Also check for\ngarbled text layers by measuring alphabetic ratio and dictionary hit rate, including the invisible OCR\nlayers that older scanners wrote, which \"have text\" but may be too poor to retrieve on. The fix is per-page triage:\nroute image-only and unusable pages to OCR with the right languages, escalate low-confidence pages, and\nreprocess from the stored raw files. Then add a coverage check per batch so empty extractions alert."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the parsing route be decided per page rather than per file?","options":["OCR engines can only process one page at a time","Hybrid PDFs mix digital and scanned pages, so a per-file decision loses one kind or overpays for the other","Per-page decisions make provenance unnecessary","Text layers are always stored per page in the PDF format"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A scanned contract with a digital cover page passes a per-file text check, and the scanned body is lost. Per-page triage extracts digital pages directly and OCRs only the image pages."},{"q":"A PDF's text layer extracts to non-empty text. What is the main remaining risk?","options":["The text is always in the wrong reading order","The file must still be OCRed to obtain confidence scores","A broken font encoding can produce text that exists but is meaningless, so it needs a quality check","Non-empty text layers cannot contain tables"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Some PDFs map glyphs to the wrong characters. The extractor returns garbage without error; checks such as alphabetic ratio, dictionary hits or replacement characters catch it before it is indexed."},{"q":"A client forbids document images from leaving their network. Which choice does this most directly constrain?","options":["Whether you can chunk documents by heading","Whether you can use a hosted cloud OCR or document-AI service instead of an engine running inside their boundary","Whether you keep page numbers for citations","Whether you can extract text layers from born-digital PDFs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Text-layer extraction and chunking can run anywhere approved, but a hosted OCR or vision service sends page images to a third party. Residency constraints push OCR and vision models inside the boundary."}]}]});
